WEBVTT

1
00:03:17.504 --> 00:03:20.302
<i>FIDEL [IN SPANISH ON TV]:
He who merits,</i>

2
00:03:21.074 --> 00:03:27.502
<i>through qualification and virtue,
the highest ranks</i>

3
00:03:28.515 --> 00:03:33.316
<i>is, however, not counted</i>

4
00:03:33.486 --> 00:03:36.455
<i>among the members
of our Central Committee.</i>

5
00:03:36.890 --> 00:03:38.551
<i>And so</i>

6
00:03:40.727 --> 00:03:45.664
<i>we only can explain it
by reading this letter</i>

7
00:03:45.832 --> 00:03:48.995
<i>from the hand and word</i>

8
00:03:49.903 --> 00:03:54.431
<i>of Comrade Ernesto Guevara.</i>

9
00:03:56.977 --> 00:04:01.437
<i>A self-explanatory letter.</i>

10
00:04:02.983 --> 00:04:04.416
<i>It says the following:</i>

11
00:04:04.584 --> 00:04:08.213
<i>"Havana, Year of Agriculture.</i>

12
00:04:08.388 --> 00:04:14.156
<i>Fidel, at this moment
I remember many things:</i>

13
00:04:14.327 --> 00:04:17.785
<i>When I met you
at María Antonia's house,</i>

14
00:04:17.964 --> 00:04:21.024
<i>when you proposed that I come along,</i>

15
00:04:21.201 --> 00:04:23.965
<i>all the tension
involved in the preparations.</i>

16
00:04:24.771 --> 00:04:29.970
<i>One day they came by
asking who to notify in case of death,</i>

17
00:04:30.143 --> 00:04:34.375
<i>and the real possibility of it
struck us all.</i>

18
00:04:36.116 --> 00:04:39.142
<i>Later we knew it was true</i>

19
00:04:39.619 --> 00:04:45.717
<i>that in a real revolution,
one either wins or dies.</i>

20
00:04:47.193 --> 00:04:49.889
<i>I feel that I have completed
the part of my duty</i>

21
00:04:50.063 --> 00:04:53.931
<i>that tied me to the Cuban
revolution in its territory,</i>

22
00:04:54.100 --> 00:04:56.125
<i>and I say farewell to you,</i>

23
00:04:56.303 --> 00:05:00.831
<i>to the comrades, to your people,
who now are mine.</i>

24
00:05:01.508 --> 00:05:04.443
<i>I formally resign my positions</i>

25
00:05:04.611 --> 00:05:07.239
<i>in the leadership of the party,</i>

26
00:05:07.414 --> 00:05:11.214
<i>my post as minister,
my rank of comandante,</i>

27
00:05:11.384 --> 00:05:13.579
<i>and my Cuban citizenship.</i>

28
00:05:14.688 --> 00:05:21.321
<i>Other nations of the world summon
my modest efforts of assistance.</i>

29
00:05:21.494 --> 00:05:26.625
<i>I can do what is denied to you
by your responsibility as Cuba's leader,</i>

30
00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:29.200
<i>and the time has come for us to part.</i>

31
00:05:30.403 --> 00:05:34.703
<i>If my last hour
arrives beneath other skies,</i>

32
00:05:34.908 --> 00:05:39.470
<i>my last thought
will be for this people,</i>

33
00:05:39.646 --> 00:05:42.410
<i>and especially for you."</i>

34
00:06:06.673 --> 00:06:09.233
FIDEL:
Let me see you without the hat.

35
00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:17.147
You're the living portrait of your father.

36
00:06:20.153 --> 00:06:22.121
You have the money?

37
00:06:23.356 --> 00:06:24.880
Yes.

38
00:06:27.994 --> 00:06:31.623
We could wait until things settle down.

39
00:06:32.999 --> 00:06:39.529
If we don't do it now,
we'll have to wait another 50 years.

40
00:06:39.706 --> 00:06:41.003
CHE:
One more.

41
00:06:41.174 --> 00:06:44.200
One, two,

42
00:06:45.612 --> 00:06:47.102
three.

43
00:06:47.881 --> 00:06:49.314
[BABY CRYING]

44
00:06:49.482 --> 00:06:53.680
ALEIDA: Aleidita, remember Mom and Dad
sit at the head of the table.

45
00:06:53.853 --> 00:06:57.448
But since Dad's not here,
we'll give his friend Ramón the seat.

46
00:06:57.924 --> 00:07:03.021
Here it is. Four and five.

47
00:07:03.196 --> 00:07:05.187
[IMITATING AIRPLANE]

48
00:07:09.536 --> 00:07:11.026
GIRL:
What is he doing?

49
00:07:11.204 --> 00:07:13.672
CHE: I don't know.
Go see what your brother is doing.

50
00:07:14.808 --> 00:07:17.106
Go see what your brother is doing.

51
00:08:13.867 --> 00:08:16.893
MAN:
Special representative from the OAS?

52
00:08:17.070 --> 00:08:21.166
Yes, the Organization
of American States.

53
00:08:22.609 --> 00:08:24.543
Just a second, please.

54
00:08:28.681 --> 00:08:30.808
There's a man who is
a special OAS representative.

55
00:08:31.184 --> 00:08:36.212
Organization of American States?
Yes, let him in.

56
00:08:41.461 --> 00:08:43.656
MAN: Please, come in.
- Thank you.

57
00:08:44.397 --> 00:08:46.524
BOY: Mister, may I help you
with your suitcase?

58
00:08:46.699 --> 00:08:48.758
No need.

59
00:08:49.602 --> 00:08:51.263
No. No.

60
00:09:27.807 --> 00:09:29.798
[SPEAKING IN SPANISH]

61
00:09:38.484 --> 00:09:40.475
[MAN SPEAKING INDISTINCTLY]

62
00:11:10.943 --> 00:11:12.934
[WATER RUNNING]

63
00:11:27.894 --> 00:11:30.488
[CHATTERING]

64
00:14:06.652 --> 00:14:09.212
MAN:
I'm worried about Monje.

65
00:14:09.455 --> 00:14:10.615
Why?

66
00:14:10.790 --> 00:14:14.453
When I asked him about
the 20 men he promised,

67
00:14:15.027 --> 00:14:19.225
he said, "Which men?"
as if I had never talked to him.

68
00:14:21.567 --> 00:14:22.932
It's okay.

69
00:14:34.881 --> 00:14:36.508
Apolinar.

70
00:14:37.750 --> 00:14:38.774
Serapio.

71
00:14:38.951 --> 00:14:41.476
CHE: How old are you, Serapio?
- Sixteen.

72
00:14:42.855 --> 00:14:45.653
At 16, a man already knows
what he wants.

73
00:14:48.694 --> 00:14:50.924
Hello. I'm Camba.

74
00:14:51.097 --> 00:14:52.462
Ramón.

75
00:14:57.570 --> 00:14:59.970
Do you know who that man is?

76
00:15:00.239 --> 00:15:01.763
He came with you.

77
00:15:01.941 --> 00:15:04.535
The man you just shook hands with

78
00:15:04.710 --> 00:15:06.735
is Che Guevara.

79
00:15:08.581 --> 00:15:10.515
[CHUCKLING]

80
00:15:10.683 --> 00:15:13.083
CAMBA: Are you sure?
- Yes.

81
00:15:14.687 --> 00:15:16.416
CAMBA:
Could I shake his hand again?

82
00:15:16.589 --> 00:15:17.886
[LAUGHS]

83
00:16:19.418 --> 00:16:21.010
[CHATTERING]

84
00:16:25.424 --> 00:16:27.153
MAN 1: Ramón.
- Ricardo.

85
00:16:30.196 --> 00:16:32.721
- This is Coco, Inti's brother.
- I know, I know.

86
00:16:32.898 --> 00:16:35.890
RICARDO: The farm is under his name.
- Braulio.

87
00:16:36.068 --> 00:16:38.935
- How are you doing, bro?
- How are you?

88
00:16:39.105 --> 00:16:41.972
MAN 2: Miguel!
MAN 3: What's up?

89
00:16:42.141 --> 00:16:46.475
- Urbano, how was the trip?
- Quite tough.

90
00:16:48.647 --> 00:16:50.638
[CHATTERING AND LAUGHING]

91
00:16:51.384 --> 00:16:53.181
CHE:
Rolando.

92
00:16:56.889 --> 00:16:57.981
You're Inti?

93
00:16:58.157 --> 00:17:02.321
- It's an honor to meet you.
CHE: The honor is mine. I'm Ramón.

94
00:17:03.629 --> 00:17:06.826
This is Dr. Ernesto Maymura.

95
00:17:06.999 --> 00:17:11.231
He's in the Bolivian Communist Party
and studied medicine in Havana.

96
00:17:11.404 --> 00:17:14.271
CHE: Where did you do your residency?
- At Calixto García.

97
00:17:14.774 --> 00:17:16.765
[CHATTERING]

98
00:17:19.211 --> 00:17:21.441
And so we fell asleep.

99
00:17:22.715 --> 00:17:24.740
Do you know where we woke up?

100
00:17:24.917 --> 00:17:28.648
- In the United States.
- In New York.

101
00:17:29.688 --> 00:17:33.249
We went right in with our
Panamanian passports.

102
00:17:33.426 --> 00:17:35.087
Everything was going smoothly

103
00:17:35.261 --> 00:17:39.459
until we almost screwed it up
because we were so hungry!

104
00:17:39.632 --> 00:17:43.762
Braulio says, "Let's go over there,
they have hot dogs."

105
00:17:43.936 --> 00:17:47.770
So we got there and, of course,
the vendor was Dominican.

106
00:17:47.940 --> 00:17:53.071
The first thing he says is,
"Oh, but you're Cuban."

107
00:17:53.245 --> 00:17:54.269
Braulio just looks at him.

108
00:17:54.447 --> 00:17:57.041
[LAUGHING]

109
00:17:57.216 --> 00:18:01.152
He served us the hot dogs
without another word.

110
00:18:01.787 --> 00:18:06.121
I made a mistake
and took the wrong flight.

111
00:18:06.292 --> 00:18:09.887
CHE: You didn't make a mistake,
you discovered something.

112
00:18:10.062 --> 00:18:11.723
What did we discover?

113
00:18:12.231 --> 00:18:15.860
If you dress an elephant as a man,
it would still make it into Bolivia.

114
00:18:16.035 --> 00:18:18.026
[LAUGHING]

115
00:18:19.405 --> 00:18:20.565
Good one!

116
00:18:20.739 --> 00:18:24.197
To Bolivia or anywhere else!

117
00:18:30.116 --> 00:18:31.310
RICARDO:
Brother!

118
00:18:41.093 --> 00:18:42.560
Let's go.

119
00:18:53.405 --> 00:18:54.770
Hello.

120
00:18:55.875 --> 00:18:59.470
- Eustaquio.
CHE: Welcome, Eustaquio. I'm Ramón.

121
00:19:00.212 --> 00:19:02.339
- Salustio.
CHE: Ramón.

122
00:19:02.515 --> 00:19:06.281
- I'm Pedro.
CHE: Ramón.

123
00:19:07.019 --> 00:19:08.384
Paco.

124
00:19:09.221 --> 00:19:12.748
- Aniceto.
CHE: Tuma, this is Aniceto.

125
00:19:13.826 --> 00:19:15.293
CARLOS:
Carlos.

126
00:19:16.228 --> 00:19:17.525
Tuma.

127
00:19:19.732 --> 00:19:21.825
- Willy.
- I'm Chapaco.

128
00:19:22.001 --> 00:19:24.799
TUMA: I'll need a few days
to learn all your names.

129
00:20:46.986 --> 00:20:48.977
[SOLDIER SPEAKING INDISTINCTLY]

130
00:20:59.932 --> 00:21:02.400
In the last conversation
I had with Monje,

131
00:21:02.568 --> 00:21:05.332
I made it clear that if we really
want to change this society,

132
00:21:05.504 --> 00:21:07.665
we are the ones
most capable of doing it.

133
00:21:07.840 --> 00:21:11.071
That's why the party
has to support the guerrilla.

134
00:21:13.479 --> 00:21:17.313
Monje expressed his doubts about
the armed struggle.

135
00:21:17.783 --> 00:21:20.251
He began to talk about
his love for his family.

136
00:21:20.419 --> 00:21:23.718
And here people also have children
and women that they have left behind.

137
00:21:25.224 --> 00:21:27.852
That is why,
with all due respect, Ramón,

138
00:21:28.661 --> 00:21:31.926
I don't think the party
will support an armed struggle,

139
00:21:32.097 --> 00:21:34.122
much less Mario Monje.

140
00:21:36.635 --> 00:21:38.796
So I want to ask for permission
to go back to La Paz

141
00:21:39.004 --> 00:21:42.804
and recruit
the best people from the party

142
00:21:43.175 --> 00:21:45.200
to join the group.

143
00:21:46.445 --> 00:21:50.381
CHE: First we have to meet
Mario Monje as we agreed to.

144
00:21:50.549 --> 00:21:53.609
Not doing so would be inappropriate.

145
00:22:09.101 --> 00:22:13.435
Conditions are not right for
the kind of struggle you propose.

146
00:22:14.340 --> 00:22:19.334
Mario, anywhere in the world where men
are being exploited by men,

147
00:22:19.511 --> 00:22:21.843
conditions are right.

148
00:22:22.614 --> 00:22:26.106
When children work in mines

149
00:22:26.552 --> 00:22:31.854
and 50 percent of miners
don't reach 30 years of age...

150
00:22:32.157 --> 00:22:35.923
When these same miners
go on strike

151
00:22:36.095 --> 00:22:38.290
to improve their wages,

152
00:22:38.464 --> 00:22:45.131
and they are massacred by the army,
are those conditions right or not?

153
00:22:45.738 --> 00:22:49.504
If infant mortality rates

154
00:22:49.675 --> 00:22:52.007
are the highest in Latin America

155
00:22:52.177 --> 00:22:55.476
because of lack of hospitals
and medical care,

156
00:22:55.748 --> 00:22:58.342
the situation is right for me.

157
00:22:59.985 --> 00:23:02.545
If we learned something in Cuba,

158
00:23:03.689 --> 00:23:08.183
it's that a popular uprising
that isn't backed by armed struggle

159
00:23:08.360 --> 00:23:11.693
has no chance of taking power.

160
00:23:13.232 --> 00:23:17.726
MONJE: When people learn that
this movement is led by a foreigner,

161
00:23:17.903 --> 00:23:20.235
they will turn their backs on you.

162
00:23:21.206 --> 00:23:24.232
You will die heroically,

163
00:23:24.743 --> 00:23:29.373
but you have no hope
of achieving victory.

164
00:23:30.716 --> 00:23:33.446
Then let's change the name of Bolivia.

165
00:23:33.619 --> 00:23:38.215
After all, Simón Bolívar
was a Venezuelan, right?

166
00:23:38.390 --> 00:23:40.119
Comrade Monje,

167
00:23:40.292 --> 00:23:43.921
your job and the party's job is to
make it clear to the people that Che,

168
00:23:44.096 --> 00:23:48.465
that Ramón, is a revolutionary
from the continent, not a foreigner.

169
00:23:48.634 --> 00:23:52.400
It's true. Ramón is like Simón Bolívar.

170
00:23:52.805 --> 00:23:56.036
MONJE: The party does not endorse
armed struggle.

171
00:23:56.408 --> 00:23:59.206
You are free to abandon it.

172
00:24:00.412 --> 00:24:03.848
And you will have our support if you do.

173
00:24:04.016 --> 00:24:09.852
If you stay, the party will no longer
pay your stipends.

174
00:24:10.656 --> 00:24:15.025
As the head of the Communist Party here,
I advise you to leave with me.

175
00:24:16.228 --> 00:24:19.425
[PEOPLE CHATTERING]

176
00:24:23.869 --> 00:24:26.963
With a mojito,
you cannot grind up the leaves.

177
00:24:27.139 --> 00:24:28.163
I know about mojitos.

178
00:24:28.340 --> 00:24:30.001
FIDEL:
So you know you can't grind them up.

179
00:24:30.175 --> 00:24:32.336
You cannot grind the leaves.

180
00:24:42.254 --> 00:24:43.846
How are you?

181
00:24:44.289 --> 00:24:47.417
Come over.
Just a second, I'm finishing here.

182
00:24:47.593 --> 00:24:49.424
Then you have to cover it.

183
00:24:49.595 --> 00:24:51.790
Half an hour in the oven
with the correct temperature.

184
00:24:51.964 --> 00:24:55.400
No, I forgot something.
On top of the cream, put cheese.

185
00:24:55.567 --> 00:24:57.057
WOMAN:
Cheese.

186
00:24:57.569 --> 00:24:59.764
Excuse us for a second.
We need to talk.

187
00:24:59.938 --> 00:25:00.962
WOMAN:
Go ahead.

188
00:25:03.141 --> 00:25:05.439
- Everything good?
RÉGIS: Yes, good.

189
00:25:06.979 --> 00:25:10.642
Why did Che change
the location of the camp?

190
00:25:11.083 --> 00:25:13.950
I think he wanted to be
closer to Argentina.

191
00:25:14.586 --> 00:25:17.680
RÉGIS: He had contacts
waiting for him in Alto Beni.

192
00:25:17.990 --> 00:25:22.359
The mines are there.
Miners are more politicized.

193
00:25:22.528 --> 00:25:24.587
They support us.

194
00:25:24.963 --> 00:25:29.127
Che needed somewhere
to train for six months

195
00:25:29.334 --> 00:25:31.529
where he wouldn't be bothered.

196
00:25:32.738 --> 00:25:36.868
How is that Ñancahuazú region?

197
00:25:37.976 --> 00:25:43.881
It's a very remote place.
Life is hard there.

198
00:25:44.249 --> 00:25:48.345
In fact, the few peasants who live there

199
00:25:48.520 --> 00:25:51.580
don't trust foreigners.

200
00:25:52.824 --> 00:25:57.523
The Soviets won't allow Monje
to make a deal.

201
00:25:57.696 --> 00:25:59.061
No.

202
00:26:01.333 --> 00:26:06.794
We have some money
that we want you to take to Bolivia.

203
00:26:07.439 --> 00:26:13.571
And we need to find out if there's
anything else that we need to send.

204
00:26:15.247 --> 00:26:18.705
We also found a cave
which could be useful.

205
00:26:19.651 --> 00:26:21.312
Good morning.

206
00:26:22.521 --> 00:26:26.457
I told the men that I'm going back
to La Paz today.

207
00:26:27.259 --> 00:26:30.126
I'm resigning from the party.

208
00:26:30.796 --> 00:26:34.425
My mission is over.

209
00:26:34.866 --> 00:26:37.926
- Okay.
- The jeep arrives this afternoon.

210
00:26:39.237 --> 00:26:42.832
CHE: Why don't you stay in the camp
until the jeep arrives?

211
00:26:43.008 --> 00:26:44.999
I would like to be alone.

212
00:26:45.677 --> 00:26:48.145
I'll wait at the zinc house.

213
00:26:48.313 --> 00:26:49.678
Fine.

214
00:26:50.048 --> 00:26:56.146
I'll return on January 10th,
with my decision made.

215
00:26:56.922 --> 00:27:00.380
Okay. Monje, we'll be here.

216
00:27:06.732 --> 00:27:10.828
Coco, find Moisés Guevara.

217
00:27:11.003 --> 00:27:12.868
Tell him I want to talk to him.

218
00:27:13.572 --> 00:27:18.305
We're leaving in a few days
on a training march

219
00:27:18.744 --> 00:27:21.804
for about 20, 25 days.

220
00:27:24.349 --> 00:27:26.214
I'll go to the mines.

221
00:27:26.718 --> 00:27:31.121
I'll be back between
the 4th and 14th of February.

222
00:27:31.289 --> 00:27:34.486
I'd like to come back earlier,
but it's Carnival season,

223
00:27:34.660 --> 00:27:36.924
La Diablada in Oruro.

224
00:27:37.663 --> 00:27:40.860
My men will be all spread out,
it'll be difficult to gather them.

225
00:27:43.335 --> 00:27:46.736
You are our primary connection
to the outside.

226
00:27:47.539 --> 00:27:50.269
Your cover needs to remain intact.

227
00:27:50.442 --> 00:27:53.036
You can't come back to the camp.

228
00:27:54.246 --> 00:27:57.579
There may be a strike at Siglo XX.

229
00:28:00.252 --> 00:28:02.083
And your husband?

230
00:28:02.354 --> 00:28:04.982
How are things?
Did you get the trip straightened out?

231
00:28:05.157 --> 00:28:09.184
I arranged everything so that
he could go study in Bulgaria.

232
00:28:09.461 --> 00:28:12.624
What about Barrientos?
What happened there?

233
00:28:13.532 --> 00:28:15.432
We've been in contact.

234
00:28:17.035 --> 00:28:20.869
- What happened?
- I think he likes me.

235
00:28:21.039 --> 00:28:23.234
I know he likes me.

236
00:28:25.077 --> 00:28:28.672
- Should I try to see him again?
- Yes.

237
00:28:29.581 --> 00:28:33.711
Tania, when this thing heats up,

238
00:28:34.286 --> 00:28:36.846
your job will make the difference.

239
00:28:40.025 --> 00:28:44.655
MAN 1: Here are provisions:
Sugar, eggs, flour, what's needed.

240
00:28:50.068 --> 00:28:54.266
The new employee, is he Bolivian?

241
00:28:54.439 --> 00:28:55.838
MAN 2:
Yes.

242
00:28:56.508 --> 00:28:58.499
- How much do I owe you?
MAN 1: No, keep your money.

243
00:28:58.677 --> 00:29:02.135
MAN 1: No, keep it.
MAN 2: We are neighbors now.

244
00:29:02.314 --> 00:29:04.839
- Take it.
MAN 1: I'm not doing this for the money.

245
00:29:10.756 --> 00:29:13.054
I want to take part in your project.

246
00:29:13.225 --> 00:29:18.185
I have relatives in transportation.
You can rely on me for everything.

247
00:29:19.264 --> 00:29:20.595
Okay?

248
00:29:21.032 --> 00:29:22.727
Go with Tuma to see our neighbor

249
00:29:22.901 --> 00:29:24.027
[COUGHS]

250
00:29:25.203 --> 00:29:29.902
and tell him we need his help.
Involve him without offering much.

251
00:29:31.343 --> 00:29:34.972
Tell him that we'll pay for
what he carries in the jeep

252
00:29:35.147 --> 00:29:36.808
and nothing else.

253
00:29:37.015 --> 00:29:38.505
Tumaini.

254
00:29:39.151 --> 00:29:40.880
Put a little fear in him.

255
00:29:41.052 --> 00:29:43.714
And explain to him that if he betrays us
he'll pay with his life.

256
00:29:45.323 --> 00:29:47.723
You can drive back and forth
to town in your jeep

257
00:29:47.893 --> 00:29:52.125
so you can buy the food we need,
and we'll pay you well.

258
00:29:52.297 --> 00:29:54.356
Yes, sure, whatever you want.

259
00:29:54.866 --> 00:29:59.064
- Good.
TUMA: I have to tell you something.

260
00:29:59.237 --> 00:30:02.832
In private, because it's something
I would only say to a friend.

261
00:30:03.008 --> 00:30:04.373
Okay, fine.

262
00:30:12.450 --> 00:30:13.508
[COCKS GUN]

263
00:30:26.031 --> 00:30:27.396
You want some more?

264
00:30:28.767 --> 00:30:30.098
[CHUCKLES]

265
00:30:30.836 --> 00:30:34.067
There were two little birds
who lived in a tree.

266
00:30:34.606 --> 00:30:39.634
One was quiet, the other liked to
fluff his feathers and sing and sing.

267
00:30:39.811 --> 00:30:43.144
One day a hunter came by.

268
00:30:43.315 --> 00:30:48.378
He heard him sing,
then put him in his sights and shot.

269
00:30:48.820 --> 00:30:51.880
But the other bird, the quiet one,
nothing happened to him.

270
00:30:52.057 --> 00:30:53.786
He saved his own life.

271
00:30:55.060 --> 00:30:56.152
It's a nice story.

272
00:30:56.328 --> 00:30:58.660
- It's good.
- It's good. I liked it.

273
00:30:58.830 --> 00:31:01.355
I really liked it. I know what you mean.

274
00:31:16.481 --> 00:31:18.915
You junkie son of a bitch.

275
00:31:19.651 --> 00:31:21.175
We'll see.

276
00:31:23.088 --> 00:31:26.751
[CAR APPROACHING]

277
00:31:35.767 --> 00:31:37.325
MAN:
Good morning.

278
00:31:37.869 --> 00:31:39.666
- How are you?
- Good morning.

279
00:31:40.238 --> 00:31:42.138
MAN:
This looks beautiful!

280
00:31:43.909 --> 00:31:46.571
I see the factory is doing well.

281
00:31:50.482 --> 00:31:53.474
- Good morning.
- Come with me.

282
00:31:55.720 --> 00:31:58.450
Can we come in? Show me the house.

283
00:32:02.227 --> 00:32:04.593
I see you are really busy.

284
00:32:07.732 --> 00:32:09.290
CHE:
Soon.

285
00:32:10.168 --> 00:32:14.537
I don't know when, but soon
we will confront the enemy.

286
00:32:16.308 --> 00:32:20.642
Our contact
with the outside will be limited.

287
00:32:22.647 --> 00:32:27.846
Some of us will die,
and it's sad to see your comrades die

288
00:32:29.821 --> 00:32:33.052
because you can't help them
with your limited resources.

289
00:32:34.326 --> 00:32:36.385
Food will be scarce.

290
00:32:37.329 --> 00:32:40.093
Sometimes we won't have
anything to eat.

291
00:32:43.101 --> 00:32:45.399
And if all of this weren't enough,

292
00:32:45.904 --> 00:32:49.965
we'll have to endure the harsh weather.

293
00:32:52.544 --> 00:32:54.034
By the end,

294
00:32:54.846 --> 00:32:57.076
we will have become human waste.

295
00:32:57.916 --> 00:33:00.077
That's all I can promise you.

296
00:33:00.852 --> 00:33:02.752
<i>Think it over.</i>

297
00:33:05.757 --> 00:33:08.726
<i>You still have time to go back home.</i>

298
00:33:08.960 --> 00:33:10.894
<i>Afterwards it will be too late.</i>

299
00:33:13.365 --> 00:33:14.832
<i>In closing,</i>

300
00:33:17.202 --> 00:33:20.501
<i>I would like to repeat</i>

301
00:33:21.606 --> 00:33:23.369
<i>what I said to Monje.</i>

302
00:33:25.210 --> 00:33:28.179
<i>Me? I'm already here.</i>

303
00:33:29.581 --> 00:33:32.379
<i>They would have to kill me
to get me out of here.</i>

304
00:33:33.151 --> 00:33:37.611
<i>I will be at the side
of those who stay, until victory.</i>

305
00:34:05.383 --> 00:34:07.374
[CLASSICAL MUSIC PLAYING
ON RADIO]

306
00:34:17.095 --> 00:34:19.325
[LAUGHING AND CHATTERING]

307
00:34:19.631 --> 00:34:22.930
SOLDIER: How much corn
can you sell us right now?

308
00:34:23.535 --> 00:34:26.026
There's not even enough for us, sir.

309
00:34:26.204 --> 00:34:28.331
At least sell us a pig.

310
00:34:28.506 --> 00:34:33.136
They're piglets, just little ones.
How am I going to sell them?

311
00:34:33.311 --> 00:34:35.245
Besides, they belong to the missus.

312
00:34:35.647 --> 00:34:38.377
How much is your pig, madam?

313
00:34:38.550 --> 00:34:42.042
I can't sell them,
they're only sucklings.

314
00:34:42.220 --> 00:34:44.848
I'll pay you 30,000 bolivianos.

315
00:34:45.523 --> 00:34:47.286
I'm not sure.

316
00:34:48.460 --> 00:34:50.360
How about 40,000?

317
00:34:50.595 --> 00:34:53.223
Should we sell or not?

318
00:34:55.066 --> 00:34:57.261
Make it 50,000, okay?

319
00:34:57.802 --> 00:34:59.201
Okay.

320
00:35:00.038 --> 00:35:01.938
CHE:
What conditions do they live in?

321
00:35:02.807 --> 00:35:04.866
Very bad.

322
00:35:06.911 --> 00:35:08.105
Do they have children?

323
00:35:08.279 --> 00:35:13.979
They have six kids.
Barefoot, all full of ticks.

324
00:35:14.152 --> 00:35:17.815
One of the little boys
has a badly infected eye.

325
00:35:17.989 --> 00:35:19.957
How old is the peasant?

326
00:35:20.425 --> 00:35:23.952
About 40, 50 years old.

327
00:35:24.129 --> 00:35:27.030
I think he's a bit older, you know?

328
00:35:29.834 --> 00:35:33.895
And when you offered the money,
how did he react?

329
00:35:34.072 --> 00:35:37.166
His eyes sparkled
when he saw the money.

330
00:35:37.509 --> 00:35:38.908
SOLDIER:
Hello.

331
00:35:40.011 --> 00:35:42.036
Good day, Honorato.

332
00:35:43.748 --> 00:35:45.739
[CHATTERING]

333
00:35:52.123 --> 00:35:54.318
Come, I want to talk to you.

334
00:35:57.996 --> 00:36:01.329
We want there
to be schools for your children,

335
00:36:01.533 --> 00:36:04.229
and hospitals to take care of them,

336
00:36:04.869 --> 00:36:06.336
and roads to be built

337
00:36:06.504 --> 00:36:09.996
so trucks can reach here,
and you can sell your products.

338
00:36:10.875 --> 00:36:13.844
Don't you want all these things?

339
00:36:14.045 --> 00:36:16.570
Well, yes, that would be nice.

340
00:36:16.748 --> 00:36:20.149
But in order to do this,
we need your help.

341
00:36:28.893 --> 00:36:32.385
CHE:
Two and three.

342
00:36:32.964 --> 00:36:34.329
Look.

343
00:36:36.568 --> 00:36:38.502
You are a big man.

344
00:36:38.670 --> 00:36:40.137
May God repay you.

345
00:36:40.305 --> 00:36:43.297
CHE: It's all right.
- May God repay you.

346
00:36:49.147 --> 00:36:50.478
CHE:
Okay.

347
00:36:51.516 --> 00:36:54.974
- See you.
SOLDIER: Bye, madam.

348
00:36:56.020 --> 00:36:58.580
- Goodbye.
- May God repay you.

349
00:37:51.175 --> 00:37:53.166
[INAUDIBLE DIALOGUE]

350
00:38:10.094 --> 00:38:11.891
What are you doing?

351
00:38:14.165 --> 00:38:16.395
That's communal food.

352
00:38:17.935 --> 00:38:20.631
No one helps themselves
without being given permission to do so.

353
00:38:20.805 --> 00:38:22.136
Understood?

354
00:38:23.675 --> 00:38:26.769
I'll let you off this one time
because you don't know the rules yet.

355
00:38:26.944 --> 00:38:31.005
- Everything belongs to the group.
- But we're hungry.

356
00:38:31.182 --> 00:38:36.085
SOLDIER: We're all hungry, Darío.
But one can feeds six for breakfast.

357
00:38:47.799 --> 00:38:49.323
There's one missing.

358
00:39:09.454 --> 00:39:13.754
I have no radio coverage with
the main camp, so I'm going back.

359
00:39:13.925 --> 00:39:17.292
No, big guy. You're not going anywhere.

360
00:39:17.462 --> 00:39:21.057
Stay with us until 6, then you can
tell Ramón how far we've gone.

361
00:39:21.232 --> 00:39:22.995
No, I'm going now
while it's still light out.

362
00:39:23.167 --> 00:39:25.658
Hey, I'm giving you an order!

363
00:39:25.837 --> 00:39:27.964
Stay here until 6.

364
00:39:30.541 --> 00:39:33.237
- I'm leaving now.
- You're not leaving, can't you hear?

365
00:39:33.411 --> 00:39:34.901
You listen to me, I'm fucking going!

366
00:39:35.079 --> 00:39:37.377
You'll have to go through
my machete first!

367
00:39:37.548 --> 00:39:39.607
- Hey!
SOLDIER: What's wrong with you?

368
00:39:39.784 --> 00:39:41.877
Hey, Miguel!

369
00:39:42.053 --> 00:39:44.044
What's going on here?

370
00:39:44.222 --> 00:39:46.156
Listen, listen!

371
00:39:46.324 --> 00:39:47.882
Miguel, look at me!

372
00:39:48.059 --> 00:39:49.924
Hey, you're comrades.

373
00:39:50.094 --> 00:39:52.756
- For what? He has to follow orders!
- That's enough!

374
00:39:53.464 --> 00:39:57.025
CHE:
Miguel, you have to control yourself

375
00:39:57.201 --> 00:40:00.398
and give orders with respect

376
00:40:00.571 --> 00:40:02.300
like a boss.

377
00:40:03.674 --> 00:40:08.168
You cannot threaten or hit anyone here.

378
00:40:08.679 --> 00:40:11.671
That's not permitted by the guerrilla.

379
00:40:11.983 --> 00:40:14.508
And you know that better than anyone.

380
00:40:16.087 --> 00:40:17.816
And you

381
00:40:19.424 --> 00:40:21.119
lied.

382
00:40:22.293 --> 00:40:27.321
Miguel never hit you with the machete
handle. He didn't even touch you.

383
00:40:29.033 --> 00:40:31.593
The one thing I won't tolerate here

384
00:40:31.769 --> 00:40:33.930
is a liar.

385
00:40:34.138 --> 00:40:36.629
One more incident like that, Pacho,

386
00:40:37.175 --> 00:40:40.406
and I'll send you back to Cuba
with a dishonorable discharge.

387
00:40:43.815 --> 00:40:47.410
Incidents like this destroy group morale
and our readiness for combat,

388
00:40:47.585 --> 00:40:49.985
not only of you or you,

389
00:40:50.154 --> 00:40:52.418
but of the entire group.

390
00:40:54.292 --> 00:40:57.352
If anyone feels weak or discouraged,

391
00:40:58.162 --> 00:41:00.926
he shouldn't resort to such methods,

392
00:41:02.233 --> 00:41:04.997
but should speak
to the political commissaries,

393
00:41:05.169 --> 00:41:07.933
to Inti or Rolando or to me,

394
00:41:08.606 --> 00:41:10.904
and we will peacefully discharge him
from the group.

395
00:41:11.242 --> 00:41:13.233
[GRUNTING]

396
00:41:41.472 --> 00:41:45.340
I didn't come here to work.
We should have a rifle.

397
00:41:51.649 --> 00:41:53.378
Listen to me.

398
00:41:54.585 --> 00:41:59.079
Ramón could be back now
in Cuba with his family,

399
00:41:59.590 --> 00:42:04.357
with many cars, enjoying comfort
everywhere he goes.

400
00:42:04.529 --> 00:42:09.364
But he is here because he wants you
to have a better life,

401
00:42:09.734 --> 00:42:12.862
so your kids can learn
to read and write.

402
00:42:13.838 --> 00:42:18.070
I'm just a baker,
I've been a slave all my life,

403
00:42:18.543 --> 00:42:20.909
but someday I'll be free.

404
00:42:26.183 --> 00:42:29.243
Rest, because we'll be
leaving at sunrise.

405
00:42:29.420 --> 00:42:32.583
MAN: We're not staying here?
- No, we need to be patient.

406
00:42:32.757 --> 00:42:36.022
We are taking you to another camp
where you'll be safer.

407
00:42:36.494 --> 00:42:38.587
Have you had any problems?

408
00:42:38.763 --> 00:42:41.789
There's a neighbor that thinks
we're making cocaine.

409
00:42:41.966 --> 00:42:42.990
[CHUCKLING]

410
00:42:43.167 --> 00:42:45.795
The police have
been here twice already.

411
00:42:51.909 --> 00:42:54.241
When's the last time
you heard from Ramón?

412
00:42:54.412 --> 00:42:56.744
Over a week ago.

413
00:42:56.914 --> 00:42:59.576
We lost contact with the whole group.

414
00:43:01.452 --> 00:43:04.387
Moisés, hello!

415
00:43:05.990 --> 00:43:07.548
[SPEAKING INDISTINCTLY]

416
00:43:08.960 --> 00:43:10.518
ARTURO:
Who did you bring?

417
00:43:10.695 --> 00:43:14.859
Contacts from Argentina and France.
Chino is from Perú.

418
00:43:17.101 --> 00:43:18.796
ARTURO:
Are you going back to Camiri?

419
00:43:18.970 --> 00:43:21.768
I need to talk to Ramón first.

420
00:43:21.939 --> 00:43:25.397
Monje and the party
are interfering with everything.

421
00:43:25.576 --> 00:43:29.637
They even convinced the last Bolivians
that were trained in Cuba

422
00:43:29.814 --> 00:43:33.648
not to come here and join us.

423
00:43:33.985 --> 00:43:35.009
[CHATTERING]

424
00:43:35.186 --> 00:43:37.620
Hey, listen.

425
00:43:37.788 --> 00:43:40.814
Hey. Everyone keep away
from her hammock.

426
00:43:40.992 --> 00:43:44.655
Whoever touches her
will be severely punished.

427
00:43:46.063 --> 00:43:48.031
Listen to me, Arturo.

428
00:43:49.433 --> 00:43:53.028
They are Bolivian,
and they have come to fight.

429
00:43:53.204 --> 00:43:55.798
You must not disrespect them.

430
00:44:04.915 --> 00:44:09.784
I need two volunteers to go hunt,
and I thought about you.

431
00:44:11.255 --> 00:44:15.385
Bring something good
because supplies are running low.

432
00:44:18.295 --> 00:44:23.528
Hey, what the hell are you doing?
We'll get money for this!

433
00:45:35.639 --> 00:45:37.436
[AIRCRAFT DRONING]

434
00:45:37.608 --> 00:45:39.599
Hey, that sounds like a plane.

435
00:45:40.811 --> 00:45:42.506
SOLDIER:
Airplane!

436
00:45:44.582 --> 00:45:46.015
Come on, Ramón.

437
00:45:52.923 --> 00:45:54.823
Get down, Ramón.

438
00:46:21.886 --> 00:46:25.185
SOLDIER: They caught two of
Moisés' men who deserted,

439
00:46:25.356 --> 00:46:26.983
and they talked like parrots.

440
00:46:27.158 --> 00:46:29.558
Soldiers went into the zinc house.

441
00:46:29.727 --> 00:46:32.491
They seized Tania's jeep and a mule.

442
00:46:32.663 --> 00:46:35.427
- Who gave orders to move camp?
- Antonio did.

443
00:46:35.599 --> 00:46:38.534
Tell him we'll win the war with bullets.

444
00:46:39.103 --> 00:46:41.401
- Bring back all to the camp.
- Okay.

445
00:46:41.572 --> 00:46:43.130
Rolando.

446
00:46:44.508 --> 00:46:46.976
Prepare an ambush.

447
00:46:53.083 --> 00:46:55.051
Was there anything in the jeep?

448
00:46:56.020 --> 00:47:00.184
I had some recordings
of Fidel's speeches.

449
00:47:01.692 --> 00:47:04.786
They're going to search the jeep
which will lead them to the hotel,

450
00:47:04.962 --> 00:47:07.954
and the hotel will lead them
to your apartment,

451
00:47:08.532 --> 00:47:11.399
and your apartment will
lead them to everything.

452
00:47:11.769 --> 00:47:14.329
I couldn't trust anyone.

453
00:47:15.706 --> 00:47:18.834
Every time I called
they hung up the phone on me.

454
00:47:19.009 --> 00:47:25.710
- That's why I decided to bring--
- You had strict orders not to return.

455
00:47:26.317 --> 00:47:31.084
That's five years of lost work.

456
00:47:31.655 --> 00:47:33.816
Tania, five!

457
00:47:34.425 --> 00:47:36.052
Give me your rifle.

458
00:47:37.928 --> 00:47:41.921
You and the visitors will leave
as soon as we prepare your trip.

459
00:48:11.929 --> 00:48:13.920
[GUNFIRE]

460
00:48:23.007 --> 00:48:24.599
MAN:
Hold your fire!

461
00:48:25.476 --> 00:48:29.936
We surrender! We surrender!

462
00:48:32.616 --> 00:48:36.211
My superiors are cowards
and don't tell us anything.

463
00:48:36.387 --> 00:48:39.845
I didn't want to be here. I swear.

464
00:48:40.057 --> 00:48:41.718
SOLDIER:
Calm down.

465
00:48:41.992 --> 00:48:45.428
- Please don't kill me, please.
SOLDIER: Calm down.

466
00:48:45.696 --> 00:48:47.220
[SPEAKING IN SPANISH]

467
00:48:48.799 --> 00:48:50.562
I suffer from rheumatism, I swear.

468
00:48:50.734 --> 00:48:52.565
SOLDIER: No one is going to hurt you.
- And I have a bad heart.

469
00:48:53.504 --> 00:48:56.837
Don't believe a word Major Plata says.
He's a liar and he's abusive.

470
00:48:57.007 --> 00:49:00.340
He sells our food to the stores
and keeps the money for himself.

471
00:49:00.511 --> 00:49:05.676
He hits people.
Look how he's crying. He's a coward.

472
00:49:06.216 --> 00:49:08.548
SOLDIER:
So, what should we do with him?

473
00:49:08.719 --> 00:49:10.687
Kill him.

474
00:49:11.588 --> 00:49:13.419
Yes, kill him.

475
00:49:15.926 --> 00:49:17.120
CHE:
Put him on his side.

476
00:49:17.294 --> 00:49:19.285
[PLATA GROANING]

477
00:49:20.030 --> 00:49:23.625
- Did you give him a painkiller?
- Not yet.

478
00:49:24.668 --> 00:49:26.135
CHE:
Give him some morphine.

479
00:49:27.171 --> 00:49:30.698
SOLDIER 1: We have committed ourselves
to a struggle in which,

480
00:49:30.874 --> 00:49:35.106
whether we like it or not,
we may have to kill

481
00:49:35.512 --> 00:49:37.571
or be killed,

482
00:49:37.748 --> 00:49:41.206
because we are fighting
for Bolivia's freedom.

483
00:49:42.353 --> 00:49:46.255
You can do it now.

484
00:49:53.397 --> 00:49:56.833
I won't be returning.
I'm quitting the army.

485
00:49:58.869 --> 00:50:02.305
SOLDIER 2:
We hope to see you soon.

486
00:50:02.840 --> 00:50:07.243
Because we could use more
of your ammunitions and weapons.

487
00:50:12.016 --> 00:50:13.847
SOLDIER 1:
Long live a free Bolivia!

488
00:50:14.018 --> 00:50:16.350
SOLDIER 2:
Long live the National Liberation Army!

489
00:50:16.520 --> 00:50:18.511
[MAN SHOUTS IN SPANISH]

490
00:50:25.729 --> 00:50:28.163
No one wanted to stay.

491
00:50:29.166 --> 00:50:30.997
That takes time.

492
00:50:47.885 --> 00:50:50.718
[IN ENGLISH]
This is a national emergency.

493
00:50:50.888 --> 00:50:53.686
Is the United States going to sit back

494
00:50:53.857 --> 00:50:56.883
and let Castro create another
Communist revolution in Bolivia,

495
00:50:57.061 --> 00:50:58.460
in the heart of South America?

496
00:50:58.629 --> 00:51:00.756
MAN 1: Mr. President,
the threat has three components.

497
00:51:00.931 --> 00:51:04.128
One, the actions
of the guerillas themselves.

498
00:51:04.301 --> 00:51:08.135
Two, the lack of anti-guerilla capability
at all levels in the field,

499
00:51:08.305 --> 00:51:09.932
including your high command.

500
00:51:10.107 --> 00:51:13.042
Three, the potential for the various
elements in the population

501
00:51:13.210 --> 00:51:15.041
to become indifferent to the situation.

502
00:51:15.212 --> 00:51:17.203
That's my point.

503
00:51:17.381 --> 00:51:19.975
What if Che Guevara is here?

504
00:51:20.150 --> 00:51:22.345
I can't have that information
made public.

505
00:51:22.519 --> 00:51:24.282
Mr. President, our latest intelligence

506
00:51:24.455 --> 00:51:26.582
indicates Guevara did not get
out of the Congo.

507
00:51:26.757 --> 00:51:29.282
Guevara is irrelevant
if you're controlling the press

508
00:51:29.460 --> 00:51:32.918
and winning the hearts of the peasants.
They're your source of information.

509
00:51:33.097 --> 00:51:35.964
If anyone's going to know
where the guerrillas are, it's them.

510
00:51:36.133 --> 00:51:38.931
MAN 2: We believe you should train
a small, specially equipped force

511
00:51:39.103 --> 00:51:41.162
to deal directly with the existing threat.

512
00:51:41.338 --> 00:51:44.034
These men should be recruited
from existing Bolivian forces

513
00:51:44.208 --> 00:51:48.076
and their area of operation
should be restricted to the combat zone.

514
00:51:49.379 --> 00:51:51.472
BARRIENTOS:
And who is going to train these men?

515
00:51:51.648 --> 00:51:55.379
We have men supervising
similar operations in Vietnam.

516
00:51:55.552 --> 00:51:57.520
They can work with your people.

517
00:51:58.455 --> 00:52:02.221
CHE [IN SPANISH]: No, if you leave now
you could be arrested.

518
00:52:02.392 --> 00:52:06.419
I think you should wait until
we start our march northward.

519
00:52:06.597 --> 00:52:09.464
- You're going north?
- Yes.

520
00:52:10.234 --> 00:52:13.965
Then we can let you off
on the Cochabamba road.

521
00:52:14.138 --> 00:52:17.835
No, I don't think that's right.
That could take two or three months.

522
00:52:18.008 --> 00:52:21.375
Ciro, we're at war.

523
00:52:22.913 --> 00:52:24.540
Give me a guide.

524
00:52:24.715 --> 00:52:27.183
Give me a guide,
and I'll make the arrangements.

525
00:52:27.351 --> 00:52:30.548
I can't break up the group
under these circumstances.

526
00:52:31.288 --> 00:52:33.688
I'll think about an alternative.

527
00:52:33.857 --> 00:52:37.418
But any move we make will be made
with the group as a whole.

528
00:52:48.272 --> 00:52:51.503
Ramón, your books are soaked.

529
00:52:51.675 --> 00:52:54.610
CHE:
Put them on those rocks to dry.

530
00:52:59.049 --> 00:53:00.448
Arturo.

531
00:53:00.617 --> 00:53:03.643
ARTURO: Water got in the radio,
everything is rusted.

532
00:53:04.321 --> 00:53:06.949
What are you saying,
that it doesn't work?

533
00:53:07.124 --> 00:53:09.991
ARTURO:
It still receives, but it doesn't send.

534
00:53:14.498 --> 00:53:16.932
Shit. Now we're really screwed.

535
00:53:17.100 --> 00:53:22.902
Not quite. We still have
the visitors to relay messages.

536
00:53:26.510 --> 00:53:30.105
MAN: The army says that you are here
to practice Communism.

537
00:53:30.280 --> 00:53:33.977
That you have no God,
that you all work for the state.

538
00:53:34.151 --> 00:53:38.247
That you rape women
and destroy families.

539
00:53:38.422 --> 00:53:42.916
You kill those who refuse to serve you
and want to make us slaves.

540
00:53:43.093 --> 00:53:45.254
And what kind of freedom do you have?

541
00:53:45.429 --> 00:53:47.158
MAN: The kind where
I can do whatever I want.

542
00:53:47.331 --> 00:53:51.427
Listen to me.
None of what they said is true.

543
00:53:51.602 --> 00:53:53.729
We haven't come to harm anybody.

544
00:53:53.904 --> 00:53:56.998
We just want to buy
some food from you, that's all.

545
00:53:57.307 --> 00:54:00.572
But I have nothing, only this.

546
00:54:02.679 --> 00:54:04.806
SOLDIER:
How much do you want for this?

547
00:54:05.549 --> 00:54:08.677
Whatever you have. Take it.

548
00:54:09.686 --> 00:54:14.316
I'll stay if you need me.

549
00:54:14.825 --> 00:54:19.592
What I need now are peasants.

550
00:54:21.198 --> 00:54:25.100
It's better if you go to France and make
a network to promote our cause,

551
00:54:25.269 --> 00:54:30.036
so they can send us money,
medicine and radios.

552
00:54:31.775 --> 00:54:35.711
I'd also need you to take
some messages to Cuba.

553
00:54:37.080 --> 00:54:39.571
I'll write Sartre and Bertrand Russell

554
00:54:39.750 --> 00:54:44.312
to organize a worldwide fund

555
00:54:44.488 --> 00:54:47.480
on behalf of the Bolivian revolution.

556
00:54:59.102 --> 00:55:01.070
CHE:
Don't worry.

557
00:55:01.371 --> 00:55:04.101
We'll take you out of here
when you recover.

558
00:55:05.876 --> 00:55:08.470
I'm not worried.

559
00:55:16.286 --> 00:55:17.776
Tumaini.

560
00:55:28.398 --> 00:55:30.127
Here, it's yours.

561
00:55:30.534 --> 00:55:32.559
To protect you.

562
00:55:42.479 --> 00:55:48.008
I'll move southward with the vanguard
and take the visitors out by Muyupampa.

563
00:55:48.652 --> 00:55:51.746
I want you to stay with the sick
until we come back.

564
00:55:51.922 --> 00:55:54.117
SOLDIER:
How long will you be gone?

565
00:55:54.291 --> 00:55:55.758
CHE:
About three days.

566
00:56:36.266 --> 00:56:37.733
CHE:
Rolando.

567
00:56:37.901 --> 00:56:41.268
Go with Loro to replace
Benigno and Miguel.

568
00:56:42.072 --> 00:56:43.403
Ramón.

569
00:56:44.174 --> 00:56:45.573
There's no one in the house.

570
00:56:45.742 --> 00:56:49.542
So leave the money with a note.

571
00:57:04.928 --> 00:57:06.919
[MEN CHATTERING]

572
00:57:10.734 --> 00:57:12.725
[MACHINE GUN FIRING IN DISTANCE]

573
00:57:14.638 --> 00:57:16.799
Pick up everything, we're leaving.

574
00:57:26.917 --> 00:57:28.350
Ramón.

575
00:57:31.388 --> 00:57:33.481
There's no sign of Loro.

576
00:57:41.331 --> 00:57:43.697
HONORATO:
That's what they've given me, captain.

577
00:57:44.100 --> 00:57:45.362
What good is this for?

578
00:57:46.069 --> 00:57:48.094
Are there any shops here?

579
00:57:49.406 --> 00:57:52.603
They took my pigs and my corn.

580
00:57:54.578 --> 00:57:58.014
You helped them,
and they gave you money.

581
00:57:58.181 --> 00:58:01.844
Now you are going to help us
eliminate them, otherwise...

582
00:58:02.018 --> 00:58:05.112
And now you are stealing
my chickens, captain.

583
00:58:05.956 --> 00:58:09.756
Listen to me closely, smartass,
really pay attention.

584
00:58:09.926 --> 00:58:14.920
Let me know immediately
if the guerrillas come back again.

585
00:58:15.098 --> 00:58:17.623
You have a good heart.

586
00:58:17.801 --> 00:58:18.825
[CHICKEN SQUAWKING]

587
00:58:19.002 --> 00:58:20.560
You'll do it.

588
00:58:33.650 --> 00:58:35.345
MAN 1 [IN ENGLISH]: Alejandro.
MAN 2: Hey.

589
00:58:35.519 --> 00:58:37.749
MAN 1: This is Captain Mitchell,
in charge of training.

590
00:58:37.921 --> 00:58:40.355
Nice to meet you.

591
00:58:50.100 --> 00:58:52.830
MITCHELL: By the time we're through,
we'll turn the Bolivians

592
00:58:53.003 --> 00:58:57.201
- into U.S.-grade special forces.
- We'll train them in the following areas:

593
00:58:57.374 --> 00:59:00.571
Weapons, communications,
demolition and intelligence.

594
00:59:00.744 --> 00:59:02.234
Also educate them politically,

595
00:59:02.412 --> 00:59:05.074
so they understand the moral
component of the operation.

596
00:59:05.248 --> 00:59:07.978
We also have one of the best
chopper pilots in Nam.

597
00:59:08.151 --> 00:59:10.244
- So how much time do you need?
- Five months.

598
00:59:18.695 --> 00:59:20.026
[WHISTLES]

599
00:59:34.778 --> 00:59:36.507
[IN SPANISH]
Good morning.

600
00:59:37.714 --> 00:59:43.778
I'm just here to write an article
on the guerrilla movement in Bolivia.

601
00:59:43.954 --> 00:59:50.416
Trying to confirm
the Bolivian army's rumors

602
00:59:50.594 --> 00:59:54.724
that Che Guevara
is the leader of the group,

603
00:59:55.098 --> 00:59:58.033
and there is a woman with him.

604
00:59:58.735 --> 01:00:01.727
This guy is a Trojan horse.

605
01:00:03.907 --> 01:00:07.001
I don't want any Cubans talking to him.

606
01:00:12.449 --> 01:00:15.418
- What's up, Darío?
- Food is ready.

607
01:00:15.585 --> 01:00:19.043
CHE:
Start serving. Children first.

608
01:00:21.558 --> 01:00:23.116
Are you from Paraguay?

609
01:00:23.293 --> 01:00:27.593
Paraguay? No way, I'm from Oruro.

610
01:00:28.832 --> 01:00:30.732
Okay, kids.

611
01:00:30.900 --> 01:00:34.028
Once you are done
you have to leave, right?

612
01:00:34.204 --> 01:00:37.640
We can't leave, because that gringo
owes us money.

613
01:00:37.807 --> 01:00:40.173
CHE:
How much does he owe you?

614
01:00:40.343 --> 01:00:42.607
BOYS [IN UNISON]:
Fifty pesos.

615
01:00:42.779 --> 01:00:44.770
[MEN LAUGHING]

616
01:00:54.991 --> 01:00:57.926
CIRO: I spoke with the Englishman
about the way out.

617
01:00:59.362 --> 01:01:02.695
He says the army knows him in Camiri.

618
01:01:04.534 --> 01:01:07.025
We could get out that way.

619
01:01:08.605 --> 01:01:10.573
You are the ones to make that decision.

620
01:01:12.509 --> 01:01:16.946
We could use our papers
to pass as reporters, no problem.

621
01:01:21.017 --> 01:01:25.579
Inti will take you up the road to Camiri.

622
01:01:26.089 --> 01:01:28.751
Try not to go through Muyupampa.

623
01:01:28.992 --> 01:01:31.620
Don't get yourselves captured.

624
01:01:31.795 --> 01:01:35.629
If that happens, remember
to always deny my presence

625
01:01:35.799 --> 01:01:40.600
and the presence of all Cubans here
at each moment and at all costs.

626
01:01:41.771 --> 01:01:45.207
DARIO: Do you have a cigarette?
- No, I don't.

627
01:01:45.375 --> 01:01:47.002
DARIO:
Goddamn it!

628
01:01:47.177 --> 01:01:50.613
Why don't you smoke?
Smoking's great.

629
01:01:50.780 --> 01:01:53.305
- But I do have some tobacco.
DARIO: Oh.

630
01:01:53.483 --> 01:01:56.008
Thanks, brother.

631
01:01:57.520 --> 01:01:58.714
Where are the Cubans?

632
01:01:58.922 --> 01:02:01.982
DARIO: Well, they should be--
- Darío, come here!

633
01:02:05.662 --> 01:02:09.496
I told you not to talk to that guy.
What did he say?

634
01:02:09.666 --> 01:02:13.158
He didn't say anything, brother.
I asked him for a cigarette.

635
01:02:13.336 --> 01:02:15.065
You're going to the back.

636
01:02:52.575 --> 01:02:55.476
MAN 1:
Freeze! Stop right there!

637
01:02:55.645 --> 01:02:58.671
MAN 2: Drop your backpacks
and show us your papers, shitheads!

638
01:03:00.984 --> 01:03:02.713
MAN 3:
Throw them on the ground.

639
01:03:03.853 --> 01:03:06.617
MAN: The Frenchman's name
is Régis Debray.

640
01:03:07.223 --> 01:03:10.420
Masquerades as a radical author,
a Marxist journalist.

641
01:03:11.161 --> 01:03:14.927
<i>Spends a lot of time in Cuba,
and is a good friend of Fidel Castro.</i>

642
01:03:15.899 --> 01:03:20.199
<i>The Argentine's name is Ciro Bustos.
A painter and also a writer.</i>

643
01:03:21.371 --> 01:03:23.931
<i>This character collected money in 1963</i>

644
01:03:24.107 --> 01:03:28.476
<i>for a local guerrilla movement
led by Jorge Massetti.</i>

645
01:03:28.745 --> 01:03:32.408
<i>Massetti was killed
by the police in Argentina in 1964.</i>

646
01:03:52.902 --> 01:03:54.893
[AIRCRAFT DRONING]

647
01:04:02.312 --> 01:04:05.804
<i>MAN [ON RADIO]: Two mercenaries, a
Frenchman and an Argentine, were killed</i>

648
01:04:05.982 --> 01:04:09.247
<i>when confronted by the army
in the area known as Vaca Guzmán.</i>

649
01:04:09.719 --> 01:04:12.711
The Guzmán region,
that's in Muyupampa.

650
01:04:16.025 --> 01:04:19.222
If that's true, those bastards
are going to pay for it, bigtime.

651
01:04:28.738 --> 01:04:32.003
The three of them were arrested.
They were taken to Camiri.

652
01:04:32.175 --> 01:04:33.233
Who told you that?

653
01:04:33.409 --> 01:04:36.378
We stopped a van
that came from Muyupampa

654
01:04:36.546 --> 01:04:41.609
with a German priest and a man.
They gave all the information.

655
01:04:41.784 --> 01:04:46.448
They want to talk to the boss.
They gave us some sweets and stuff.

656
01:04:46.756 --> 01:04:49.350
- Inti, have those people seen you?
- No.

657
01:05:00.970 --> 01:05:03.268
SCHWARTZ:
Many of your men are not Bolivian.

658
01:05:03.439 --> 01:05:06.135
That's why no one trusts you.

659
01:05:06.309 --> 01:05:08.072
But they trust you.

660
01:05:08.244 --> 01:05:12.738
MAN:
They asked us to talk to you.

661
01:05:13.249 --> 01:05:17.549
Then tell them that our army
is open to anyone who wants to fight.

662
01:05:17.720 --> 01:05:19.449
They should be here with us.

663
01:05:19.622 --> 01:05:23.114
No, they reject everything
that has to do with violence.

664
01:05:23.293 --> 01:05:27.821
INTI: So people in power can use
violence to oppress us,

665
01:05:27.997 --> 01:05:30.727
but we can't use it to free ourselves?

666
01:05:30.900 --> 01:05:33.562
SCHWARTZ: They don't want to join you,
and they don't want you here.

667
01:05:33.736 --> 01:05:37.297
You don't have the right
to ruin their lives.

668
01:05:44.080 --> 01:05:46.412
You are going to have peace today
in Muyupampa

669
01:05:46.582 --> 01:05:49.915
on the condition that you bring us
a truck of food and medical goods

670
01:05:50.119 --> 01:05:52.815
by 6 p.m. on this watch.

671
01:05:53.423 --> 01:05:57.450
We'll meet at Nemesio's farm.
We'll pay for everything.

672
01:05:58.027 --> 01:06:01.087
Then we'll go, you have my word.

673
01:06:02.065 --> 01:06:03.089
Okay.

674
01:06:03.266 --> 01:06:04.995
[MOANING]

675
01:06:05.168 --> 01:06:07.102
Open your mouth.

676
01:06:26.556 --> 01:06:29.184
They will torture Bustos and Debray.

677
01:06:29.359 --> 01:06:31.486
They'll learn everything.

678
01:06:36.866 --> 01:06:38.390
Ramón.

679
01:06:39.068 --> 01:06:42.003
I think you should change your name.

680
01:06:43.539 --> 01:06:45.700
CHE:
That's not a bad idea.

681
01:06:45.908 --> 01:06:47.899
[AIRCRAFT DRONING]

682
01:06:51.681 --> 01:06:54.172
Plane! Get everyone out of the house.

683
01:06:54.350 --> 01:06:56.250
- Run!
SOLDIER 1: Cover yourselves!

684
01:06:57.186 --> 01:06:58.813
SOLDIER 2:
Quickly, under the tree!

685
01:06:58.988 --> 01:07:00.512
Get out of here!

686
01:07:07.730 --> 01:07:10.221
Most of these men are Cubans.
Don't be mistaken.

687
01:07:12.272 --> 01:07:16.971
This is an invasion promoted by Castro.

688
01:07:19.647 --> 01:07:24.914
A Communist plot to remove a peaceful,
democratically elected government.

689
01:07:25.085 --> 01:07:27.849
MAN: President Barrientos,
what about the Americans?

690
01:07:28.022 --> 01:07:31.219
Are there U.S. military advisors?

691
01:07:31.392 --> 01:07:35.795
BARRIENTOS: The United States has sent
planes, helicopters and nothing more.

692
01:07:35.963 --> 01:07:39.763
That's their response
to the guerrilla threat?

693
01:07:40.367 --> 01:07:46.033
Our military is entirely capable
of handling this situation.

694
01:07:46.940 --> 01:07:50.137
The guerrilla forces
are on their last legs.

695
01:07:51.345 --> 01:07:54.041
REPORTER:
Is Che Guevara leading this group?

696
01:07:54.782 --> 01:07:59.412
I'll say it once.
Che Guevara is not in Bolivia.

697
01:08:13.033 --> 01:08:15.661
- Fernando. Fernando.
- Hm.

698
01:08:15.836 --> 01:08:19.135
The miners are threatening
to go on strike.

699
01:08:21.875 --> 01:08:23.137
Where?

700
01:08:23.310 --> 01:08:25.437
In Catavi, Siglo XX.

701
01:08:25.779 --> 01:08:28.111
The miners are going on strike!

702
01:08:28.916 --> 01:08:30.907
[MEN CHATTERING AND CHUCKLING]

703
01:08:55.442 --> 01:09:00.436
Fernando. Miners have pledged 50
bolivianos of their paychecks to our cause.

704
01:09:02.583 --> 01:09:05.347
For them, that's a fortune.

705
01:09:07.621 --> 01:09:10.647
Arturo, report to Miguel at the ambush

706
01:09:10.824 --> 01:09:12.917
and replace Pablito.

707
01:09:14.962 --> 01:09:17.658
I'll go through all of Bolivia
without firing a single shot.

708
01:09:17.831 --> 01:09:19.230
Okay.

709
01:09:21.468 --> 01:09:23.732
MAN 1: Sir, the situation is slipping
through our fingers.

710
01:09:24.204 --> 01:09:26.570
The pope and de Gaulle
are pressuring us about Debray.

711
01:09:26.740 --> 01:09:30.767
Some members of your coalition
are expressing support for the strike

712
01:09:30.944 --> 01:09:32.878
and sympathy for the rebel cause.

713
01:09:33.113 --> 01:09:36.014
We need to take control of this thing.

714
01:09:36.450 --> 01:09:38.782
What do you have in mind?

715
01:09:38.952 --> 01:09:40.180
Tell people about Guevara.

716
01:09:40.354 --> 01:09:44.085
Declare a state of emergency
and crack down on the Siglo XX miners,

717
01:09:44.258 --> 01:09:45.748
the iron fist.

718
01:09:45.926 --> 01:09:50.158
Seal the borders and declare
a national state of war.

719
01:09:50.497 --> 01:09:52.226
And the rebels?

720
01:09:52.432 --> 01:09:53.956
MAN 2:
Your main problem, Mr. President,

721
01:09:54.134 --> 01:09:57.763
is the lack of coordination
among your deployed forces.

722
01:09:57.938 --> 01:10:00.463
I couldn't agree more, sir.

723
01:10:01.742 --> 01:10:03.403
MAN 3:
What do you suggest?

724
01:10:03.944 --> 01:10:05.741
Divide the region in three zones.

725
01:10:05.913 --> 01:10:09.576
Surround each zone,
cut off its source of supply,

726
01:10:09.750 --> 01:10:12.685
and liquidate any guerrilla forces.

727
01:10:16.757 --> 01:10:19.055
<i>MAN [ON RADIO]: A few minutes ago,
Radio Argentina reported</i>

728
01:10:19.226 --> 01:10:22.252
<i>the sad news about
the Siglo XX massacre,</i>

729
01:10:22.462 --> 01:10:26.364
<i>which resulted in the deaths
of 87 miners.</i>

730
01:10:26.533 --> 01:10:30.435
<i>The families and fellow miners
mourn their loss.</i>

731
01:10:30.604 --> 01:10:33.437
What are they saying
on Bolivian stations?

732
01:10:33.807 --> 01:10:35.434
The same.

733
01:10:35.609 --> 01:10:39.636
They confirm the battle in the mines,
but they are silent about the death toll.

734
01:10:42.216 --> 01:10:44.207
[RAPID GUNSHOTS IN DISTANCE]

735
01:10:56.663 --> 01:10:58.528
They hit Pombo.

736
01:10:59.800 --> 01:11:02.030
They almost got us, man.

737
01:11:09.543 --> 01:11:11.534
[GRUNTING]

738
01:11:21.388 --> 01:11:22.946
CHE:
Pour me some water.

739
01:11:35.736 --> 01:11:37.601
Come on, breathe.

740
01:11:55.856 --> 01:11:57.153
The worst is over.

741
01:12:09.770 --> 01:12:11.761
[SPEAKING INDISTINCTLY]

742
01:12:22.316 --> 01:12:26.719
SOLDIER: He asked that you hold
his watch until you can give it to his son.

743
01:12:39.032 --> 01:12:40.522
Joaquín.

744
01:12:41.068 --> 01:12:43.366
We can't circle it forever.

745
01:12:43.537 --> 01:12:46.005
This place is crawling with troops.

746
01:12:48.475 --> 01:12:50.875
Ramón told us
not to move out of the area.

747
01:12:51.044 --> 01:12:54.775
That was before
the army overran our camp.

748
01:12:54.948 --> 01:12:57.678
We have no food left, nothing.

749
01:12:57.851 --> 01:13:00.684
If Ramón were coming back,
he'd be here by now.

750
01:13:01.555 --> 01:13:04.183
SOLDIER: If we leave,
how are we going to meet him?

751
01:13:04.358 --> 01:13:08.351
If Ramón is not here it's because
the army is preventing him.

752
01:13:09.196 --> 01:13:12.529
I say we find our way to the Rio Grande.

753
01:13:12.699 --> 01:13:16.795
We've got as much chance
finding Ramón across the river

754
01:13:16.970 --> 01:13:19.404
as we do here.

755
01:13:28.615 --> 01:13:30.606
[MACHINE GUN FIRING IN DISTANCE]

756
01:13:37.224 --> 01:13:38.851
RICARDO:
Good morning, miss.

757
01:13:39.026 --> 01:13:41.460
Can you sell me a can of milk?

758
01:13:43.096 --> 01:13:45.087
Ricardo, what's up?

759
01:13:45.799 --> 01:13:49.064
- Shut up and give me your canteen.
- Moron!

760
01:14:02.382 --> 01:14:04.873
SOLDIER 1:
Breathe! Now hold it.

761
01:14:05.052 --> 01:14:06.679
- Fuck!
SOLDIER 2: Calm down.

762
01:14:06.853 --> 01:14:08.150
Hold it!

763
01:14:26.573 --> 01:14:27.597
SOLDIER:
Breathe.

764
01:14:46.660 --> 01:14:49.788
How long are we going
to look for these people?

765
01:14:50.397 --> 01:14:52.228
SOLDIER:
Until we find them.

766
01:14:52.399 --> 01:14:56.733
I agree, but I think
we're moving away from them.

767
01:14:56.903 --> 01:15:00.168
We should try to make contact
with the capital again.

768
01:15:00.340 --> 01:15:02.570
We're at an impasse.

769
01:15:02.742 --> 01:15:06.007
How many more of us have to die
to find the other group?

770
01:15:06.179 --> 01:15:11.515
SOLDIER: Chapaco, if you were lost,
we'd do the same thing.

771
01:15:11.751 --> 01:15:15.915
No one abandons anyone in the group.

772
01:15:16.456 --> 01:15:17.821
Is that clear?

773
01:15:20.293 --> 01:15:21.726
Let's go.

774
01:15:30.604 --> 01:15:32.595
[CHE WHEEZING]

775
01:15:58.398 --> 01:16:00.389
[COUGHING]

776
01:16:13.380 --> 01:16:15.439
Excuse me, Fernando,

777
01:16:17.751 --> 01:16:20.117
but the men are starving,

778
01:16:20.387 --> 01:16:22.753
and we have nothing to give them.

779
01:16:23.023 --> 01:16:27.790
We also have nothing for the animals.
They're eating the bark off the trees.

780
01:16:27.961 --> 01:16:30.122
- What do you want me to do?
- Kill a horse.

781
01:16:37.871 --> 01:16:39.839
I'm giving you another shot.

782
01:17:11.805 --> 01:17:14.137
Fernando, with your permission.

783
01:17:19.112 --> 01:17:22.047
I think I could

784
01:17:22.215 --> 01:17:26.174
make it back to the camp
to look for your medicines.

785
01:17:27.587 --> 01:17:29.077
CHE:
What do you want?

786
01:17:29.823 --> 01:17:31.882
Do you want me
to divide the group again?

787
01:17:33.026 --> 01:17:34.687
I'm not doing it.

788
01:17:34.861 --> 01:17:39.855
And even less, risk anyone's life

789
01:17:40.033 --> 01:17:41.728
for the sake of my own well-being.

790
01:18:44.664 --> 01:18:48.862
CHE:
We are in a difficult situation.

791
01:18:49.202 --> 01:18:52.365
The truth is, all of us

792
01:18:52.539 --> 01:18:54.530
have made mistakes.

793
01:18:55.175 --> 01:18:57.370
But I made the worst mistake

794
01:18:57.811 --> 01:19:01.247
when I didn't bring my medicines along.

795
01:19:01.981 --> 01:19:05.417
This struggle gives us

796
01:19:06.219 --> 01:19:08.847
the opportunity to become

797
01:19:09.456 --> 01:19:15.019
true revolutionaries,
the highest level of humanity.

798
01:19:16.062 --> 01:19:18.496
To become men

799
01:19:18.865 --> 01:19:23.063
in the purest sense of the word.

800
01:19:25.238 --> 01:19:27.502
Too old to learn.

801
01:19:28.842 --> 01:19:31.504
If the wheel rolls downhill,

802
01:19:31.678 --> 01:19:33.737
let it go.

803
01:19:34.746 --> 01:19:37.306
[GUNFIRE]

804
01:19:52.230 --> 01:19:54.095
[IN SPANISH]
Come on, let's go up.

805
01:19:57.569 --> 01:19:59.264
Let's go!

806
01:20:05.310 --> 01:20:08.609
- Let's go!
- No, I can hold them!

807
01:20:08.780 --> 01:20:10.304
Go!

808
01:20:15.253 --> 01:20:16.880
You bastards!

809
01:20:17.055 --> 01:20:20.855
[MAN SPEAKING SPANISH ON RADIO]

810
01:20:22.460 --> 01:20:26.556
They're reporting three hours
of intense combat in Monteagudo

811
01:20:26.731 --> 01:20:30.258
between a group of guerrillas
and the 3rd division.

812
01:20:32.971 --> 01:20:34.962
The army didn't suffer any casualties.

813
01:20:35.140 --> 01:20:36.903
Here's Monteagudo.

814
01:20:38.677 --> 01:20:40.770
They are talking about
one dead guerrilla

815
01:20:40.945 --> 01:20:42.640
named Antonio Fernández from Tarata.

816
01:20:42.814 --> 01:20:45.681
Pombo, sounds like it was Pedro.

817
01:20:45.850 --> 01:20:47.647
He's from Tarata.

818
01:20:48.753 --> 01:20:50.948
Monteagudo is here.

819
01:20:51.990 --> 01:20:55.721
- Where exactly?
- Here, south of Ñancahuazú.

820
01:21:03.168 --> 01:21:04.931
[CHE COUGHING]

821
01:21:05.103 --> 01:21:08.504
This is where Joaquín
and his group are operating.

822
01:21:10.208 --> 01:21:12.768
- So, what we're hearing makes sense.
- We're not far off.

823
01:21:17.882 --> 01:21:20.180
- Good afternoon, sir.
- Good afternoon.

824
01:21:20.351 --> 01:21:23.081
With your permission,
I need to check your house.

825
01:21:24.122 --> 01:21:25.919
- Good afternoon.
- Good afternoon.

826
01:21:28.093 --> 01:21:29.685
JOAQUÍN:
Hello, madam.

827
01:21:31.029 --> 01:21:32.656
WOMAN:
Good afternoon.

828
01:21:39.804 --> 01:21:41.965
JOAQUÍN: Don't worry, madam,
we won't do you any harm.

829
01:21:43.675 --> 01:21:45.575
Who's that man over there?

830
01:21:46.411 --> 01:21:48.641
- Sit down.
- No, thanks.

831
01:21:49.314 --> 01:21:52.477
- Who is that man?
MAN: Oh, he's a friend...

832
01:21:52.650 --> 01:21:53.810
Is he all right?

833
01:21:54.018 --> 01:21:56.384
JOAQUÍN:
Have you seen the army around here?

834
01:21:56.988 --> 01:22:00.185
WOMAN:
He's a neighbor. He has a fever.

835
01:22:00.992 --> 01:22:03.153
We have a doctor with us.

836
01:22:03.328 --> 01:22:07.731
We'll bring him to see you
when we come back.

837
01:22:07.999 --> 01:22:09.193
Do you understand me?

838
01:22:09.367 --> 01:22:11.164
JOAQUÍN:
But what about the army?

839
01:22:13.371 --> 01:22:16.169
We need to find a way
across the river, a ford.

840
01:22:16.341 --> 01:22:19.606
You live here and you know
this area well. You can help us.

841
01:22:23.381 --> 01:22:25.246
Get us some food.

842
01:22:25.483 --> 01:22:27.280
There, have some money.

843
01:22:27.452 --> 01:22:30.353
MAN: You are seven people, right?
And eight with the woman.

844
01:22:32.190 --> 01:22:34.055
JOAQUÍN:
How do you know that we're seven?

845
01:22:35.460 --> 01:22:39.521
TANIA: I'm slowing everyone down.
I don't know if I'll be able to go on.

846
01:22:39.697 --> 01:22:42.257
JOAQUÍN:
Of course you can, Tania.

847
01:22:43.034 --> 01:22:45.434
We'll be back in two days.

848
01:22:48.807 --> 01:22:53.506
Remember, sir, find us an easy place
to cross the river.

849
01:23:52.237 --> 01:23:55.035
Pombo, I've got to talk to Fernando.

850
01:23:55.206 --> 01:23:58.369
Fernando is sleeping.
He's recovering from an asthma attack.

851
01:23:58.543 --> 01:24:01.535
Don't you think he's had
enough worries for today?

852
01:24:01.713 --> 01:24:02.975
Let him rest.

853
01:24:03.147 --> 01:24:05.615
CHE:
Inti, what's the matter?

854
01:24:06.251 --> 01:24:07.946
POMBO:
Okay, go and tell him.

855
01:24:13.825 --> 01:24:17.158
I think you should speak
to Camba and Chapaco.

856
01:24:17.328 --> 01:24:21.355
Camba says his physical condition
prevents him from continuing.

857
01:24:22.066 --> 01:24:24.534
I think he no longer sees
the point of the struggle.

858
01:24:26.971 --> 01:24:28.768
I talked to Inti already.

859
01:24:30.074 --> 01:24:34.738
Fernando, I am a bit sick.

860
01:24:34.913 --> 01:24:36.642
- I don't believe--
- Camba.

861
01:24:37.749 --> 01:24:42.015
I can't let you go
until we meet Joaquín's group,

862
01:24:42.820 --> 01:24:45.050
because if they capture you,

863
01:24:45.223 --> 01:24:47.418
you not only risk your own life,

864
01:24:47.592 --> 01:24:51.926
but you also put the whole group at risk.

865
01:24:53.598 --> 01:24:55.395
Look, Camba.

866
01:24:55.566 --> 01:24:58.535
To survive here, to win,

867
01:24:59.304 --> 01:25:02.102
you have to live
as if you've already died.

868
01:25:11.049 --> 01:25:14.041
I never said to Inti that I wanted to quit.

869
01:25:15.119 --> 01:25:17.314
Quitting is for cowards.

870
01:25:19.190 --> 01:25:24.025
What I told Inti is that in six months
to a year, I want to return home.

871
01:25:35.873 --> 01:25:37.397
Let's see.

872
01:25:39.143 --> 01:25:40.804
Tell me

873
01:25:42.981 --> 01:25:46.246
exactly where you said to cross.

874
01:25:47.485 --> 01:25:51.581
MAN: Just about here is where
I will make them cross, captain.

875
01:25:58.997 --> 01:26:00.521
Now,

876
01:26:02.000 --> 01:26:06.403
put this white shirt on
so we don't shoot at you.

877
01:26:07.905 --> 01:26:11.238
Now go home and don't move

878
01:26:11.409 --> 01:26:16.142
until the guerrillas arrive,
or they'll suspect something.

879
01:28:06.991 --> 01:28:08.481
Fire!

880
01:28:15.500 --> 01:28:17.491
<i>MAN [IN SPANISH ON RADIO]:
Today, the Bolivian army</i>

881
01:28:17.668 --> 01:28:21.570
<i>reported a rebel clash
at Vado del Yeso.</i>

882
01:28:21.739 --> 01:28:24.970
<i>Ten rebels were reported killed.</i>

883
01:28:25.143 --> 01:28:29.079
<i>The group's one survivor,
José Carrillo, is a local miner</i>

884
01:28:29.247 --> 01:28:33.240
<i>who was under the command
of a Cuban named Joaquín Acuña.</i>

885
01:28:33.417 --> 01:28:35.612
CHE:
That's bullshit from the army.

886
01:28:35.786 --> 01:28:37.845
They can't all be dead,

887
01:28:38.389 --> 01:28:41.119
unless they were
walking in their sleep.

888
01:28:41.459 --> 01:28:43.359
INTI:
José Carrillo is Paco.

889
01:28:43.528 --> 01:28:46.224
Maybe he was caught and is talking.

890
01:28:48.299 --> 01:28:49.323
CHE:
Let's go.

891
01:28:51.035 --> 01:28:52.468
It's already 5:00.

892
01:28:56.641 --> 01:28:59.542
[CAMERAS CLICKING]

893
01:29:03.414 --> 01:29:05.439
[CAMERAS CLICKING]

894
01:29:13.257 --> 01:29:16.158
- What happened to her face?
- The piranhas ate it.

895
01:29:18.362 --> 01:29:21.627
I requested a coffin.
There's a cemetery in town.

896
01:29:21.799 --> 01:29:24.029
Mr. President,
with your permission, sir.

897
01:29:24.202 --> 01:29:27.330
What do you plan to do with Che
if he is captured alive?

898
01:29:29.373 --> 01:29:31.341
Personally, I think

899
01:29:31.509 --> 01:29:34.103
the biggest mistake Batista made

900
01:29:34.278 --> 01:29:37.179
was not killing Fidel
when he had the chance.

901
01:30:40.011 --> 01:30:41.444
Thanks.

902
01:30:44.248 --> 01:30:49.584
Fernando, they say the truck comes
every day, and the deputy--

903
01:30:49.754 --> 01:30:51.779
- What's his name?
- Vidal Aguilar.

904
01:30:51.956 --> 01:30:55.289
--left yesterday for Vallegrande
to warn the army about us.

905
01:30:55.459 --> 01:30:59.122
It's a two-day-old telegram
sent by the army to the deputy.

906
01:30:59.297 --> 01:31:02.630
It says, "Any expenses incurred will be

907
01:31:02.800 --> 01:31:06.099
reimbursed by the army."

908
01:31:06.304 --> 01:31:09.205
Inti is already talking to the wife.
They own a store.

909
01:31:09.373 --> 01:31:11.705
Tell Inti that in reprisal for that,

910
01:31:11.876 --> 01:31:14.208
we will take all that we need
from the store.

911
01:31:14.679 --> 01:31:19.082
Take 10 cans of sardines
and those tomatoes.

912
01:31:21.252 --> 01:31:23.846
WOMAN:
This is for my children!

913
01:31:24.021 --> 01:31:26.455
INTI:
Madam, madam.

914
01:31:26.624 --> 01:31:32.221
<i>INTI: Dear comrades, we've been here
fighting the army for six months now.</i>

915
01:31:32.430 --> 01:31:33.954
<i>You may wonder why.</i>

916
01:31:34.498 --> 01:31:38.491
<i>We want to end injustice
in this country.</i>

917
01:31:39.236 --> 01:31:42.364
<i>To end neglect and misery</i>

918
01:31:42.540 --> 01:31:45.373
<i>to which the government subjects
its own people.</i>

919
01:31:46.544 --> 01:31:50.036
<i>For example,
where is the nearest hospital?</i>

920
01:31:50.414 --> 01:31:54.282
<i>About 100 kilometers away, right?
What if you get sick?</i>

921
01:31:54.485 --> 01:31:57.613
<i>First you have to pay
to take a truck to Vallegrande,</i>

922
01:31:57.788 --> 01:31:59.312
<i>and then you pay for the stay.</i>

923
01:31:59.523 --> 01:32:02.549
<i>So you go to a hospital,
which they say is public,</i>

924
01:32:02.727 --> 01:32:05.423
<i>but to see a doctor,
you must pay first.</i>

925
01:32:06.130 --> 01:32:08.121
<i>Isn't this the way it is?</i>

926
01:32:08.532 --> 01:32:13.333
<i>And the medicine? They won't even
give you aspirin for free.</i>

927
01:32:13.504 --> 01:32:14.994
<i>In other words,</i>

928
01:32:15.439 --> 01:32:20.741
<i>if your kid gets sick or dies because
you have no money for the hospital,</i>

929
01:32:20.911 --> 01:32:22.742
<i>who is going to care?</i>

930
01:32:23.614 --> 01:32:26.378
That is the reason why your luck
changing for the better

931
01:32:26.550 --> 01:32:30.646
depends on the triumph
of our struggle...

932
01:32:31.589 --> 01:32:32.715
Yes?

933
01:32:32.890 --> 01:32:35.620
Is there going to be fighting
here in the village?

934
01:32:38.129 --> 01:32:42.623
We have no intention of
fighting in the villages.

935
01:33:28.145 --> 01:33:29.840
Nothing.

936
01:33:30.247 --> 01:33:32.306
Two pigs and a mule.

937
01:33:32.750 --> 01:33:35.275
- Weird, isn't it?
- You're telling me.

938
01:33:35.519 --> 01:33:38.977
- Where is Miguel?
- He should already be here.

939
01:33:41.959 --> 01:33:43.290
Hey.

940
01:33:43.761 --> 01:33:46.662
Madam, madam!

941
01:33:47.531 --> 01:33:48.862
Madam!

942
01:33:55.372 --> 01:33:56.964
I don't see anything.

943
01:33:57.808 --> 01:33:59.776
Absolute silence.

944
01:34:00.578 --> 01:34:03.979
Smells like soldiers to me.

945
01:34:04.648 --> 01:34:07.082
It's completely deserted.

946
01:34:07.251 --> 01:34:12.587
I talked to an old woman who said
that her grandson saw soldiers yesterday.

947
01:34:14.792 --> 01:34:16.157
CHE:
Miguel.

948
01:34:17.461 --> 01:34:21.591
Get the vanguard ready to go
to Jagüey at 1300 hours.

949
01:34:21.765 --> 01:34:24.165
POMBO: You heard it.
The vanguard, get ready.

950
01:34:24.335 --> 01:34:27.771
CHE:
Miguel, stay on guard.

951
01:34:27.938 --> 01:34:30.771
Take all security measures.

952
01:34:31.375 --> 01:34:32.774
MIGUEL:
Understood.

953
01:34:39.750 --> 01:34:41.809
CHE: Tell me.
- Fernando.

954
01:34:41.986 --> 01:34:44.079
I think it's better that I stay here.

955
01:34:44.255 --> 01:34:47.486
I can't stand up or pee.

956
01:34:48.893 --> 01:34:51.259
We aren't going to leave you here.

957
01:34:52.663 --> 01:34:57.191
When we get to Jagüey,
we'll make a decision.

958
01:35:12.516 --> 01:35:13.983
Good afternoon.

959
01:35:14.151 --> 01:35:16.085
I met him on the way to Jagüey.

960
01:35:16.253 --> 01:35:18.448
He says he comes from Vallegrande.

961
01:35:20.691 --> 01:35:22.625
Where are you going with these goods?

962
01:35:22.793 --> 01:35:27.093
To Picacho, sir.
I have jerky and cheese to sell to

963
01:35:27.264 --> 01:35:28.788
Pombo, Pombo.

964
01:35:29.099 --> 01:35:30.327
support my family, sir.

965
01:35:30.501 --> 01:35:34.870
- Have you seen the army around?
- No, sir. I haven't seen the army.

966
01:35:35.039 --> 01:35:37.337
INTI [WHISPERING]:
Fernando, he looks too nervous to me.

967
01:35:37.508 --> 01:35:39.908
CHE:
Maintain vigilance.

968
01:35:40.511 --> 01:35:43.571
Fall into line. Everyone, stay alert.

969
01:35:46.817 --> 01:35:49.911
Are you sure
you haven't seen the army?

970
01:35:50.087 --> 01:35:52.317
MAN:
No, sir. I haven't seen the army.

971
01:35:52.856 --> 01:35:54.153
CHE:
Good.

972
01:35:54.959 --> 01:35:57.587
Pombo, walk with him
to the outskirts of the village.

973
01:35:57.761 --> 01:35:59.160
MAN:
Thank you, sir.

974
01:36:09.907 --> 01:36:12.239
You have a pebble factory in your shoe.

975
01:36:12.409 --> 01:36:14.138
It's the second time
you've stopped for the same shit.

976
01:36:20.684 --> 01:36:24.848
Pacho, that way.
Darío, go over there.

977
01:36:26.123 --> 01:36:28.216
SOLDIER:
Willy, go with them.

978
01:36:28.392 --> 01:36:30.553
[GUNFIRE]

979
01:36:30.728 --> 01:36:32.252
CHE: Antonio.
POMBO: Chapaco, let's go.

980
01:36:32.429 --> 01:36:35.956
CHE:
Urbano, go over by the fence.

981
01:36:38.302 --> 01:36:40.463
Arturo, over there.

982
01:36:40.804 --> 01:36:42.237
POMBO:
Keep going, Darío.

983
01:36:43.741 --> 01:36:46.869
Retreat!
Everybody run down the hill!

984
01:36:47.044 --> 01:36:49.137
[GUNFIRE]

985
01:36:50.114 --> 01:36:53.277
I'm covering you!
Downhill, everyone!

986
01:36:53.450 --> 01:36:57.181
- Chino, what's wrong?
CHINO: I lost my glasses!

987
01:36:57.588 --> 01:36:59.453
POMBO:
They'll kill you. Here they are!

988
01:36:59.623 --> 01:37:02.183
They killed Coco, Julio and Miguel.

989
01:37:03.627 --> 01:37:06.460
Camba, this way. Camba!

990
01:37:07.097 --> 01:37:10.589
Camba! What are you doing?

991
01:37:12.236 --> 01:37:13.726
Come on!

992
01:37:13.904 --> 01:37:15.565
[GUNFIRE]

993
01:37:32.690 --> 01:37:34.214
What happened?

994
01:37:37.461 --> 01:37:40.123
They killed Miguel,
Julio and your brother.

995
01:37:44.368 --> 01:37:46.336
I tried to get them out,

996
01:37:47.271 --> 01:37:48.738
but I couldn't.

997
01:37:55.779 --> 01:37:57.246
I'm sorry.

998
01:38:14.231 --> 01:38:16.358
CAPTAIN:
This is Che's vanguard.

999
01:38:16.867 --> 01:38:18.494
COLONEL:
You think he's close?

1000
01:38:19.002 --> 01:38:22.267
CAPTAIN: I think you should bring in
the 2nd Ranger division.

1001
01:38:22.606 --> 01:38:24.972
COLONEL:
They haven't finished training.

1002
01:38:25.909 --> 01:38:28.469
CAPTAIN: You're going to need
every man you can get.

1003
01:38:59.143 --> 01:39:00.838
POMBO:
Benigno.

1004
01:39:01.779 --> 01:39:03.337
Benigno.

1005
01:39:04.281 --> 01:39:08.115
Benigno,
Fernando is asking you to stop.

1006
01:39:11.722 --> 01:39:13.155
Hold on.

1007
01:39:30.307 --> 01:39:34.471
Benigno, let me see your wound.

1008
01:39:34.645 --> 01:39:38.206
It's okay. It's nothing.

1009
01:39:41.485 --> 01:39:42.884
Take it.

1010
01:39:45.823 --> 01:39:47.848
Tell me, Fernando.

1011
01:39:49.493 --> 01:39:52.087
Go with Benigno to explore.

1012
01:39:52.696 --> 01:39:57.599
Let's see if we can find a place
to spend the day without being seen.

1013
01:40:01.805 --> 01:40:04.273
Captain, a message from
Lieutenant Huerta.

1014
01:40:04.441 --> 01:40:08.104
A peasant saw some rebels
heading toward Yuro Ravine.

1015
01:40:08.278 --> 01:40:10.246
Tell him we're on our way.

1016
01:40:20.290 --> 01:40:21.314
Look.

1017
01:40:26.463 --> 01:40:29.626
Must be a peasant
on his way to Pucará. Tsk, tsk, tsk.

1018
01:41:33.497 --> 01:41:36.261
- How many are there?
- More than a battalion.

1019
01:41:36.433 --> 01:41:39.994
POMBO: Are they together?
- Two or three meters from each other.

1020
01:41:40.170 --> 01:41:43.503
Willy, go look for Ñato
and Urbano, immediately.

1021
01:41:43.674 --> 01:41:46.700
Arturo, bring Darío and Aniceto.

1022
01:41:47.644 --> 01:41:51.876
Fernando, the left ravine
ends in a house

1023
01:41:52.049 --> 01:41:54.210
that's taken by the army.

1024
01:41:54.551 --> 01:41:58.282
- You can't get out of the middle one.
BENIGNO: This is the steepest ravine.

1025
01:41:58.555 --> 01:42:01.080
It's 500 meters to the hill.

1026
01:42:01.258 --> 01:42:05.888
And there's also a tree I think
would be good as a lookout.

1027
01:42:06.063 --> 01:42:07.758
- Then go there immediately.
- Okay.

1028
01:42:07.931 --> 01:42:12.595
Benigno, be vigilant, find a place
where we can leave during the night.

1029
01:42:12.769 --> 01:42:13.929
BENIGNO:
Okay.

1030
01:42:14.104 --> 01:42:15.571
Take care.

1031
01:42:17.641 --> 01:42:20.405
CHE:
We'll wait there until 9 at night.

1032
01:42:20.711 --> 01:42:22.440
Those who don't arrive by then

1033
01:42:22.612 --> 01:42:26.946
should make their way down
the ravine to Rio Grande,

1034
01:42:27.517 --> 01:42:30.645
and from there
to the mouth of Santa Elena.

1035
01:42:30.821 --> 01:42:33.289
We'll wait there for two days.

1036
01:42:34.391 --> 01:42:36.689
Pacho and Willy.

1037
01:42:36.860 --> 01:42:39.693
You will take this position here.

1038
01:42:39.863 --> 01:42:44.061
Antonio and Arturo,

1039
01:42:45.135 --> 01:42:47.126
into this ravine.

1040
01:42:47.337 --> 01:42:50.932
No one shoots until they shoot.

1041
01:42:51.108 --> 01:42:52.575
If you hear gunfire,

1042
01:42:52.909 --> 01:42:55.605
first, find out for sure
that they know about you

1043
01:42:55.779 --> 01:42:58.043
and that they're not just
sweeping the area.

1044
01:42:58.215 --> 01:43:01.116
Do you understand? Go now.

1045
01:43:06.089 --> 01:43:09.217
Go with Darío
and reinforce Benigno's position.

1046
01:43:09.393 --> 01:43:13.227
Don't let him fall asleep
from exhaustion.

1047
01:43:18.635 --> 01:43:20.034
POMBO:
Fernando.

1048
01:43:22.239 --> 01:43:25.140
We've been in worse
situations than this.

1049
01:43:29.079 --> 01:43:33.175
SOLDIER: There are three ravines:
La Tusca, San Antonio and Yuro.

1050
01:43:33.350 --> 01:43:36.513
The guerrillas
could be in any one of them.

1051
01:43:36.686 --> 01:43:38.415
Search all three.

1052
01:43:38.588 --> 01:43:40.852
Start in the upper canyons
and make your way down.

1053
01:43:41.024 --> 01:43:42.889
What if they try to escape?

1054
01:43:43.060 --> 01:43:45.927
I'll set up a command post
where the three ravines meet.

1055
01:45:48.718 --> 01:45:51.346
[SHOUTING & GUNFIRE IN DISTANCE]

1056
01:45:52.189 --> 01:45:53.486
[WHISPERING]
Ñato.

1057
01:45:55.458 --> 01:45:57.050
Get Aniceto.

1058
01:46:05.502 --> 01:46:08.903
Go with Ñato and take over
for Pombo and Urbano.

1059
01:46:09.072 --> 01:46:11.768
Make them both come back.

1060
01:46:12.576 --> 01:46:14.441
Wait a moment.

1061
01:46:51.648 --> 01:46:54.082
[GUNFIRE]

1062
01:46:55.285 --> 01:46:57.116
[EXPLOSION]

1063
01:46:59.055 --> 01:47:00.613
[GUNFIRE]

1064
01:47:11.268 --> 01:47:12.530
[GUNSHOT]

1065
01:47:23.380 --> 01:47:24.711
[GUNSHOT]

1066
01:47:35.292 --> 01:47:36.691
[GUNSHOTS]

1067
01:47:39.663 --> 01:47:41.221
What's that?

1068
01:47:44.334 --> 01:47:46.199
Hey, Urbano.

1069
01:47:46.670 --> 01:47:49.002
Ñato is making signs
I don't understand.

1070
01:47:49.172 --> 01:47:52.835
Either he's saying Fernando wants us
to retreat or move toward him.

1071
01:47:58.948 --> 01:48:02.111
[GUNFIRE]

1072
01:48:10.293 --> 01:48:11.658
[EXPLOSION]

1073
01:48:13.997 --> 01:48:16.830
- Antonio, do we move?
- No, we stay here.

1074
01:48:17.000 --> 01:48:18.661
[EXPLOSION]

1075
01:48:26.142 --> 01:48:29.839
CHE:
Pablito, prepare the wounded to leave.

1076
01:48:31.014 --> 01:48:32.447
Go now.

1077
01:48:46.730 --> 01:48:49.164
[SHOUTING & GUNFIRE]

1078
01:49:40.216 --> 01:49:42.446
POMBO:
Fuck! We have to leave now, Urbano.

1079
01:49:42.619 --> 01:49:45.417
URBANO:
Pombo, go now! I can cover you!

1080
01:49:45.588 --> 01:49:47.681
Wait, wait, wait!

1081
01:49:48.725 --> 01:49:51.660
Wait! Now move!

1082
01:50:05.809 --> 01:50:09.575
There they are.
Pombo and Urbano are there.

1083
01:50:26.529 --> 01:50:29.726
[GUNFIRE]

1084
01:51:43.806 --> 01:51:46.001
[GRUNTING]

1085
01:52:43.399 --> 01:52:45.196
How do you feel?

1086
01:52:46.603 --> 01:52:47.968
CHE:
Fine.

1087
01:52:49.038 --> 01:52:51.097
The lieutenant bandaged it for me.

1088
01:52:54.744 --> 01:52:56.939
Where have you taken
Willy and Chino?

1089
01:52:57.647 --> 01:52:59.376
They're next door.

1090
01:53:01.117 --> 01:53:03.517
How many guerrillas are left out there?

1091
01:53:04.420 --> 01:53:05.944
CHE:
I don't know.

1092
01:53:07.657 --> 01:53:09.887
What was your rendezvous point?

1093
01:53:10.960 --> 01:53:12.723
CHE:
We didn't have one.

1094
01:53:13.630 --> 01:53:15.325
What's your rendezvous point?

1095
01:53:15.498 --> 01:53:18.956
CHE: I told you we didn't have one.
- You didn't have one?

1096
01:53:20.003 --> 01:53:22.631
Why did you come here
to La Higuera during the day?

1097
01:53:22.805 --> 01:53:25.330
CHE: Does it matter?
Nothing matters anymore.

1098
01:53:25.508 --> 01:53:27.305
Listen to me.

1099
01:53:28.011 --> 01:53:30.138
You are no longer in Cuba, sir.

1100
01:53:31.147 --> 01:53:34.605
And you should know that
I am a colonel in the Bolivian army.

1101
01:53:34.984 --> 01:53:39.148
You escaped the Congo, Venezuela,
but I assure you, not Bolivia.

1102
01:53:40.089 --> 01:53:44.321
This is where your adventures end,
you foreign piece of shit.

1103
01:53:44.794 --> 01:53:47.126
All right. Get some rest.

1104
01:53:47.296 --> 01:53:51.392
Tomorrow we'll go to Vallegrande
to have your wound examined.

1105
01:53:57.173 --> 01:53:58.970
Put on your best face.

1106
01:53:59.876 --> 01:54:02.470
A lot of people
will want a photograph of you.

1107
01:54:04.681 --> 01:54:06.876
Maybe we should shave you.

1108
01:54:13.923 --> 01:54:15.151
[CHE SPITS]

1109
01:54:16.059 --> 01:54:17.993
Piece of shit!

1110
01:54:23.166 --> 01:54:26.602
Bring me the dead,
and leave them here, quickly!

1111
01:55:09.679 --> 01:55:11.374
[DOOR OPENS]

1112
01:55:31.934 --> 01:55:33.231
That's not right.

1113
01:55:34.270 --> 01:55:36.636
They shouldn't do that with the dead.

1114
01:55:43.513 --> 01:55:45.140
Do you want to smoke?

1115
01:55:46.282 --> 01:55:47.749
Yes.

1116
01:55:53.723 --> 01:55:55.190
Thanks.

1117
01:55:59.061 --> 01:56:00.756
How is Cuba?

1118
01:56:02.899 --> 01:56:05.060
Cuba is progressing.

1119
01:56:06.335 --> 01:56:08.303
Do they have religion there?

1120
01:56:09.972 --> 01:56:13.305
Yes, there are religions.

1121
01:56:13.876 --> 01:56:16.902
I thought Communists
didn't believe in God.

1122
01:56:17.847 --> 01:56:19.314
Well,

1123
01:56:20.316 --> 01:56:22.716
in Cuba there's no official religion,

1124
01:56:22.885 --> 01:56:24.614
but many people believe in God.

1125
01:56:26.823 --> 01:56:28.051
You don't believe in anything?

1126
01:56:28.691 --> 01:56:31.717
Yes, I believe in mankind.

1127
01:56:38.267 --> 01:56:41.065
- Are you married?
- No, no.

1128
01:56:41.237 --> 01:56:43.865
- How about you?
- Yes.

1129
01:56:44.607 --> 01:56:45.938
Do you have children?

1130
01:56:46.108 --> 01:56:48.406
- I have five.
- Five!

1131
01:56:49.579 --> 01:56:51.513
Three girls and two boys.

1132
01:56:55.318 --> 01:56:56.945
You want some more?

1133
01:56:57.720 --> 01:56:59.051
Yes.

1134
01:57:07.763 --> 01:57:09.253
What's your name?

1135
01:57:09.432 --> 01:57:10.899
Eduardo.

1136
01:57:11.701 --> 01:57:12.998
Eduardo.

1137
01:57:17.840 --> 01:57:19.808
Would you untie me, Eduardo?

1138
01:57:51.173 --> 01:57:52.504
Hey.

1139
01:57:52.909 --> 01:57:56.174
- Che asked me to free him.
- What?

1140
01:57:56.412 --> 01:57:58.073
He asked me to untie him.

1141
01:57:58.247 --> 01:58:01.011
No, you're crazy. They'd kill both of us.

1142
01:58:03.386 --> 01:58:05.377
I don't want to go back in.

1143
01:58:05.554 --> 01:58:07.283
You take the next shift.

1144
01:58:13.796 --> 01:58:16.629
[HELICOPTER WHIRRING]

1145
01:58:49.765 --> 01:58:51.960
RAMÍREZ: What are you
thinking about, comandante?

1146
01:58:53.235 --> 01:58:54.964
Where are you from?

1147
01:58:55.471 --> 01:58:57.063
RAMÍREZ:
From Cuba.

1148
01:58:58.641 --> 01:59:00.836
CHE:
I don't talk to traitors.

1149
01:59:02.411 --> 01:59:04.675
RAMÍREZ: Okay, so you don't
want to talk to traitors.

1150
01:59:05.081 --> 01:59:07.481
But listen to what I have to say.

1151
01:59:08.117 --> 01:59:10.483
You executed my uncle.

1152
01:59:13.456 --> 01:59:15.856
Must be hard, comandante?

1153
01:59:16.025 --> 01:59:18.289
You, here, in the jungle,

1154
01:59:18.461 --> 01:59:21.919
and Fidel in Havana
having lunch at El Nacional.

1155
01:59:23.733 --> 01:59:25.633
Can you imagine?

1156
01:59:53.662 --> 01:59:57.621
What made you think the Bolivians
would be receptive to your ideas?

1157
01:59:58.200 --> 02:00:00.998
We already had our revolution.

1158
02:00:01.404 --> 02:00:03.838
Didn't you hear
about the land reform in 1952?

1159
02:00:04.006 --> 02:00:09.410
Yes, and Barrientos sold out.
Now you have a military dictatorship.

1160
02:00:09.779 --> 02:00:12.475
Have you seen how your peasants live?

1161
02:00:13.282 --> 02:00:16.376
If the peasants are so bad off,

1162
02:00:16.552 --> 02:00:18.417
why did they turn you in?

1163
02:00:19.355 --> 02:00:21.619
Maybe they believed your lies.

1164
02:00:25.394 --> 02:00:28.261
Or maybe, they never wanted you here.

1165
02:00:28.431 --> 02:00:30.797
Yes, maybe.

1166
02:00:31.867 --> 02:00:34.768
Or maybe our failure
will wake them up.

1167
02:00:47.583 --> 02:00:48.811
[CAMERA CLICKS]

1168
02:01:03.666 --> 02:01:04.690
Captain.

1169
02:01:04.867 --> 02:01:06.801
- Tell me.
- A call for Colonel Zenteno.

1170
02:01:06.969 --> 02:01:11.133
I told them he had left, but they
asked for the senior officer.

1171
02:01:14.443 --> 02:01:16.035
Bravo 1.

1172
02:01:17.913 --> 02:01:19.676
This is Captain Ramírez.

1173
02:01:22.284 --> 02:01:24.184
Repeat, please.

1174
02:01:25.988 --> 02:01:29.287
Order 600. I will tell the colonel.

1175
02:01:45.241 --> 02:01:46.731
RAMÍREZ:
Put him there.

1176
02:01:48.878 --> 02:01:52.211
Take a picture.
You just have to press here.

1177
02:01:55.551 --> 02:01:57.678
So that you can smoke, comandante.

1178
02:02:00.589 --> 02:02:02.454
Say cheese.

1179
02:02:05.161 --> 02:02:06.219
[CAMERA CLICKS]

1180
02:02:09.398 --> 02:02:10.865
Stop there.

1181
02:02:18.040 --> 02:02:22.136
I have a message for you from the
high command. They ordered a 600.

1182
02:02:23.679 --> 02:02:25.579
Fine. I have to leave for Vallegrande.

1183
02:02:25.748 --> 02:02:30.708
I'll send a helicopter back at 1400 hours.
Have the body ready.

1184
02:02:31.587 --> 02:02:33.054
Yes, sir.

1185
02:02:33.289 --> 02:02:34.756
Let's go.

1186
02:02:35.691 --> 02:02:39.787
RAMÍREZ: You can shoot from here down.
Nothing more.

1187
02:02:40.062 --> 02:02:42.189
SERGEANT:
Listen to me closely.

1188
02:02:42.565 --> 02:02:44.863
I need two volunteers.

1189
02:02:50.439 --> 02:02:51.997
Someone else?

1190
02:02:57.880 --> 02:03:00.144
Sergeant. Let me do it.

1191
02:03:01.984 --> 02:03:03.975
SERGEANT:
Shoot him below the neck.

1192
02:03:05.087 --> 02:03:06.315
Come.

1193
02:03:19.335 --> 02:03:20.359
Come with me.

1194
02:03:32.081 --> 02:03:33.639
Sit down!

1195
02:03:36.719 --> 02:03:39.552
Shoot. Do it.

1196
02:03:47.229 --> 02:03:48.423
[CHE GRUNTING]

1197
02:03:52.101 --> 02:03:53.432
[GUNSHOT]

1198
02:03:53.836 --> 02:03:54.996
[GUNSHOT]

1199
02:03:56.105 --> 02:03:58.801
[CHE GASPING]
